<!-- ============================ PART 1 ============================ -->

      <div class="page-break"></div>
      <h1 style="color:#0b1f3a;">Part 01 — Project at a Glance &amp; Architecture</h1>
      <p style="color:#40525e; font-size:10pt;">Purpose, technology choices, folder layout, request lifecycle and the complete route map.</p>
    

<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section first">
  <h2>1.1 Project Overview</h2>
  <div class="route" style="font-family:Consolas; font-size:9pt; color:#0f766e;">Project name: Mentor Lab</div>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q1. What is Mentor Lab?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Mentor Lab is a full-stack education website for college students, mainly covering BCA, BBA and Biotechnology. It brings syllabus material, study notes, previous-year question papers, recorded lectures, mock tests, performance analytics, certificates, an AI study assistant and a browser code compiler into one platform.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q2. What is the main objective of the project?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The objective is to give students a single place to learn, practise and track progress. Instead of searching for notes, PYQs and mock tests separately, a student can study a subject, take a test, review mistakes, see analytics and earn a course-completion certificate. Administrators can upload content and manage paid mock-test access from one dashboard.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q3. Who are the users of the system?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">There are three practical roles: <strong>guest</strong> (can browse home, syllabus, notes, PYQ, chatbot and roadmaps), <strong>student</strong> (must log in for mock tests, analytics, profile and certificates), and <strong>admin</strong> (separate admin login, dashboard, content uploads and purchase management). Admin is a flag on the same User model, not a separate collection.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q4. Which courses are supported?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Three course families: <strong>BCA</strong> (Programming Languages, Logical Subjects, Mathematics Subjects), <strong>BBA</strong> (six management/finance/marketing categories) and <strong>Biotechnology</strong> (Foundation Sciences, Core Biotechnology, Advanced &amp; Applied). Each course has a syllabus page, category pages and a roadmap page.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q5. What is the frontend technology stack?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">React 19 with Vite 8, React Router DOM v7, Axios for HTTP requests, Recharts for dashboard charts, qrcode.react for the registration QR code, Firebase SDK for an unused Google-auth scaffold, and plain CSS files stored under <code>frontend/src/css_files</code>. The Vite React Compiler plugin is also enabled.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q6. What is the backend technology stack?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Node.js with Express 5 in ES-module mode, Mongoose 9 for MongoDB, JSON Web Tokens for authentication, bcryptjs for password hashing, Multer for uploads, pdf-parse for text extraction, Nodemailer for reset-code email, Razorpay for optional online payments, and native <code>fetch</code> calls to the Groq chat-completions API.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q7. Which database is used and why?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">MongoDB is used through Mongoose. Its document model suits course content, question arrays, activity lists and flexible note metadata. Large PDF files are not stored inside normal documents; they are stored in MongoDB GridFS buckets named <code>pdfs</code> and <code>pyqs</code>.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q8. Is this a MERN stack project?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Yes, in practice it is a MERN-style project: MongoDB, Express, React and Node.js. It is not limited to the classic MERN libraries because it also uses Vite, React Router, GridFS, Razorpay and an external AI API.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q9. Why was React chosen for the frontend?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">React allows the large number of pages to be built as reusable components, keeps the interface reactive without page reloads, and works naturally with React Router for a single-page application. It also has a large ecosystem for charts, QR codes and HTTP requests.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q10. Why was Vite used instead of Create React App?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Vite gives much faster development startup and hot-module replacement, a smaller and simpler configuration, and an optimised production build. The project's <code>vite.config.js</code> also defines a development proxy for <code>/api</code> and <code>/uploads</code>, which avoids CORS issues during local development.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q11. Why is Express used on the server?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Express provides a small, well-understood routing layer. Each feature has its own router file (<code>authRoutes</code>, <code>notesRoutes</code>, <code>mockTestRoutes</code>, and so on), which keeps the backend modular. Express also serves the built React files and uploaded PDFs from the same server.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q12. Explain the overall architecture.</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It is a three-tier architecture. The browser runs the React SPA; it calls REST endpoints on the Express server; the server validates the request, applies JWT authentication where required, and reads or writes MongoDB. External services are called only from the backend or, for the compiler, directly from the browser.</p>
    

  <div class="diagram">
    <div class="node"><strong>Presentation</strong>React 19 + Vite<br>React Router + Axios<br>Browser</div>
    <div class="arrow">→</div>
    <div class="node"><strong>Application</strong>Node.js + Express 5<br>REST API + JWT<br>Business logic</div>
    <div class="arrow">→</div>
    <div class="node"><strong>Data</strong>MongoDB + Mongoose<br>GridFS PDF buckets<br>Users, tests, results</div>
  </div>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q13. What is the folder structure of the project?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The main folder is <code>Monitor Lab</code>. It contains <code>backend</code> and <code>frontend</code>. The backend has <code>src</code> (entry and database), <code>routes</code>, <code>controllers</code>, <code>model</code>, <code>middleware</code>, <code>utils</code> and <code>uploads</code>. The frontend has <code>src/pages</code>, <code>src/components</code>, <code>src/data</code>, <code>src/css_files</code>, <code>src/context</code>, <code>src/firebase</code> and <code>src/utils</code>.</p>
    

<pre>Monitor Lab/
  backend/
    src/index.js          Express entry + route mounting
    src/db.js             MongoDB connection
    routes/               auth, admin, mocktest, notes, pyq,
                          ai, payment, certificate
    controllers/          authController, aiController, ttsController
    model/                User, MockTest, Result, Purchase, Notes,
                          PYQ, Certificate, Setting, Course, Lecture
    middleware/           protect (JWT verification)
    utils/                generateToken, mailer
    uploads/              temporary PDFs and payment QR image
  frontend/
    src/App.jsx           Router and route list
    src/pages/            one component per page
    src/components/       Chatbot, CodeCompiler, route guards
    src/data/             BCA / BBA / Biotech category data
    src/css_files/        page-wise CSS
    src/context/          dark / light theme
    src/firebase/         optional Google-auth scaffold</pre>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q14. Explain the lifecycle of a typical API request.</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">A React page calls Axios. In development, Vite proxies <code>/api</code> to <code>http://localhost:5000</code>. Express matches the router, runs middleware such as <code>protect</code>, executes the route handler, queries MongoDB through Mongoose, and returns JSON. The React component stores the response in state and re-renders. In production, Express serves the built <code>frontend/dist</code> files and the same API.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q15. How does the development proxy work?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;"><code>vite.config.js</code> maps <code>/api</code> and <code>/uploads</code> to <code>http://localhost:5000</code>. The browser therefore sends requests to the Vite dev server, which forwards them to Express. This makes frontend code use relative paths such as <code>/api/auth/login</code> and avoids hard-coded backend URLs for most requests.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q16. How is the website served in production?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">After <code>npm run build</code>, Express serves <code>frontend/dist</code> as static files. A catch-all route <code>app.get("/{*path}")</code> sends <code>index.html</code> for any non-API path, so refreshing a client-side route such as <code>/mock-test</code> still loads the React app instead of returning a 404.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q17. What is a single-page application?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">An SPA loads one HTML document and lets JavaScript change the view. React Router swaps page components without a full page reload. The server only needs to return <code>index.html</code> for frontend routes; the API returns JSON separately.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q18. Which environment variables does the backend use?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The main variables are <code>PORT</code>, <code>MONGO_URI</code>, <code>JWT_SECRET</code> and <code>GROQ_API_KEY</code>. Optional integrations use <code>RAZORPAY_KEY_ID</code>, <code>RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET</code>, and SMTP settings such as <code>SMTP_HOST</code>, <code>SMTP_USER</code> and <code>SMTP_PASS</code>. Secrets are kept in <code>.env</code> and loaded with <code>dotenv</code>; they should never be committed or printed in a report.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q19. How do you run the project locally?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Start MongoDB, create <code>backend/.env</code>, then run <code>npm install</code> and <code>npm run dev</code> (or <code>node src/index.js</code>) inside <code>backend</code>. In a second terminal, run <code>npm install</code> and <code>npm run dev</code> inside <code>frontend</code>. Vite usually opens on port 5173 and proxies API calls to port 5000.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q20. Which third-party services are integrated?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Groq for AI chat and note explanation, Google Translate TTS for text-to-speech, Razorpay for optional online payments, Nodemailer SMTP for reset codes, MongoDB Atlas or a local MongoDB server for data, and Wandbox for online code compilation. Firebase is installed and configured with placeholders but is not connected to any login button.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q21. Why are PDFs stored in GridFS instead of a normal folder?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Study notes and PYQ papers can be large and numerous. GridFS splits a file into chunks inside MongoDB, so it can handle files larger than the 16 MB BSON limit and keeps file metadata together with the database. It also allows streaming the PDF back to the browser without depending on a local filesystem path.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q22. How does dark mode work?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;"><code>ThemeProvider</code> stores a boolean <code>darkMode</code> (default <code>true</code>) in React context. <code>App.jsx</code> applies the class <code>dark-theme</code> or <code>light-theme</code> to the root div, and the CSS variables change accordingly. The Navbar's theme button toggles the context value.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q23. What are the most reusable frontend components?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The Navbar, the floating Chatbot, <code>CodeCompiler</code>, <code>ProtectedRoute</code>, <code>AdminRoute</code>, and the three course-category page components. The category pages share the same pattern but are separate files because each course has its own data source and styling.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q24. What is the complete route map of the frontend?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The public routes are home, BCA, BBA, biotech syllabus, category pages, study notes, PYQ, recorded lectures, about, contact, roadmaps, chatbot, login, signup, forgot password and note viewer. The protected routes are mock test, test review, analytics, profile (page-level guard via API), certificates and certificate view. The admin route is guarded by <code>AdminRoute</code>.</p>
    

  <table border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="3" width="100%">
