    <tr><td valign="top"><code>/profile</code></td><td valign="top">Student profile</td><td valign="top">Logged-in student</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top"><code>/certificates</code>, <code>/certificate/:id</code></td><td valign="top">Certificates</td><td valign="top">Logged-in student</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top"><code>/chatbot</code></td><td valign="top">Full-page AI assistant</td><td valign="top">Public</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top"><code>/admin</code></td><td valign="top">Admin dashboard</td><td valign="top">Admin only (client guard)</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>

  <div class="tip">
    <strong>Viva tip:</strong> If the examiner asks "what is your project in one line", say:
    "Mentor Lab is a MERN-based learning platform where BCA, BBA and Biotech students can study notes, attempt mock tests, track performance and earn certificates, while admins manage content and payments."
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================ PART 2 ============================ -->

      <div class="page-break"></div>
      <h1 style="color:#0b1f3a;">Part 02 — All Logins &amp; Authentication</h1>
      <p style="color:#40525e; font-size:10pt;">Every way a user can enter, register, recover an account or be blocked from a page.</p>
    

<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section first">
  <h2>2.1 The Complete Login Inventory</h2>
  <div class="route" style="font-family:Consolas; font-size:9pt; color:#0f766e;">Active logins: student + admin &nbsp;|&nbsp; Recovery: email OTP &nbsp;|&nbsp; Scaffolded: Google</div>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q25. How many login methods does the project actually use?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">There are <strong>two active login portals</strong>: the student login at <code>/login</code> and the admin login at <code>/admin-login</code>. Both call the same backend endpoint, <code>POST /api/auth/login</code>; the admin portal additionally checks the <code>isAdmin</code> flag. There is also an <strong>email OTP password-recovery flow</strong>, a <strong>signup flow</strong>, a <strong>QR shortcut to signup</strong>, and an <strong>unused Firebase Google-auth scaffold</strong>.</p>
    

  <table border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="3" width="100%">
    <tbody><tr><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">Access method</th><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">Entry point</th><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">Credential / action</th><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">Current status</th></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Student login</td><td valign="top"><code>/login</code></td><td valign="top">Email + password</td><td valign="top">Fully active; returns JWT</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Admin login</td><td valign="top"><code>/admin-login</code></td><td valign="top">Email + password + admin flag</td><td valign="top">Fully active; same API, extra role check</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Student signup</td><td valign="top"><code>/signup</code></td><td valign="top">Name, email, phone, password</td><td valign="top">Active; creates account and JWT</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">QR registration</td><td valign="top">Home page QR</td><td valign="top">Opens <code>/signup?ref=qr</code></td><td valign="top">Active marketing shortcut, not a separate login</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Forgot password</td><td valign="top"><code>/forgot-password</code></td><td valign="top">Email OTP then new password</td><td valign="top">Active; SMTP optional, dev OTP fallback</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Google login</td><td valign="top"><code>firebase.js</code></td><td valign="top">Google provider</td><td valign="top">Scaffolded only; placeholder config, no UI button wired</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Logout</td><td valign="top">Navbar</td><td valign="top">Clears local storage</td><td valign="top">Active</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q26. Is admin a separate login system?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">No. Admin uses the same User collection and the same login endpoint. The difference is the boolean <code>isAdmin</code> field on the user document. The admin login page rejects a valid student account with the message "Access denied. You do not have administrative privileges."</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q27. Which routes are protected on the frontend?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;"><code>ProtectedRoute</code> wraps mock test, test review, analytics, certificates and certificate view. It only checks whether a token exists in <code>localStorage</code>. <code>AdminRoute</code> wraps the admin dashboard and checks both <code>token</code> and <code>isAdmin === "true"</code>. Profile and Note Viewer are not wrapped, but they call protected APIs or behave differently without a token.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q28. What is authentication versus authorization in this project?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;"><strong>Authentication</strong> proves identity: login checks email and password and issues a JWT. <strong>Authorization</strong> decides what the identity may do: <code>protect</code> verifies the token, <code>isAdmin</code> checks decide admin actions, and <code>hasAccess</code> decides whether a student has paid for a mock test.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q29. Which backend middleware performs authentication?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;"><code>middleware/authMiddleware.js</code> exports <code>protect</code>. It reads the <code>Authorization</code> header, expects the format <code>Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code>, verifies the token with <code>JWT_SECRET</code>, loads the user by the token's <code>id</code> without the password, and attaches it as <code>req.user</code>.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q30. What happens if the token is missing or invalid?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">If no token is present, the API returns <code>401</code> with "Not authorized, no token". If verification fails, it returns <code>401</code> with "Not authorized, token failed". The frontend route guard only checks token presence, so an expired token is usually discovered when the first protected API call fails.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q31. Which endpoints are public and which require a token?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Public: signup, login, forgot/verify/reset password, notes listing and note detail, note PDF, PYQ listing and PDF, AI chat/explain/TTS, and payment settings. Protected: profile, add activity, mock tests and results, purchases, note read tracking, certificate generation and listing, and admin purchases. Some admin statistics and student-list endpoints are currently public, which is a known improvement area.</p>
    

  <h3>2.2 Student Login</h3>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q32. Explain the student login flow step by step.</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The student opens <code>/login</code>, enters email and password, and submits the form. The page sends <code>POST /api/auth/login</code> with Axios. The controller finds the user by email, explicitly selects the hidden password field, compares the password using <code>bcrypt.compare</code>, generates a JWT, and returns the user's id, name, email, <code>isAdmin</code> and token. The frontend stores the token and user in <code>localStorage</code> and navigates to the home page or the intended page.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q33. Which API endpoint handles student login and what does it return?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;"><code>POST /api/auth/login</code>. On success it returns <code>{ _id, name, email, isAdmin, token }</code>. On failure it returns <code>400</code> with "Invalid email or password". The same message is used for an unknown email and a wrong password to avoid revealing which one is wrong.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q34. How is the password verified?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Passwords are never stored in plain text. Signup hashes the password with bcrypt using 10 salt rounds. Login fetches the hash with <code>.select("+password")</code> because the schema marks password as <code>select: false</code>, then calls <code>bcrypt.compare(enteredPassword, storedHash)</code>.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q35. Why use bcrypt instead of SHA-256 for passwords?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">SHA-256 is fast and designed for integrity, which makes brute-force attacks cheaper. bcrypt is intentionally slow, uses a per-password salt, and has a configurable cost factor. The project uses 10 rounds, so each guess is computationally expensive for an attacker.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q36. What is inside the JWT?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The token is signed with <code>JWT_SECRET</code> and has the payload <code>{ id: user._id }</code> with an expiry of <strong>7 days</strong>. It does not contain the password or the admin flag. The server reloads the user from the database on every protected request, so a deleted user cannot keep using an old token.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q37. Where is the JWT stored and how is it sent?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It is stored in browser <code>localStorage</code> under the key <code>token</code>, along with a JSON copy of the user and, for admins, <code>isAdmin = "true"</code>. Protected requests send it as <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code>. Note that <code>localStorage</code> is readable by JavaScript, so a strong Content Security Policy and XSS protection are important.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q38. What does the login page do with the returned user?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It stores <code>token</code> and <code>user</code> in local storage, updates the UI state, and navigates to the home page. The Navbar then detects the token and shows the logged-in menu instead of Login/Signup. If the user came from a protected page, the app may redirect back to that page.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q39. Does the login page validate the email format and password length?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The form uses an <code>input type="email"</code> and HTML <code>required</code> attributes, and the submit handler prevents empty submissions. The backend performs the authoritative credential check. The strongest password policy is applied during password reset, not during signup.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q40. What is the difference between the student and admin login UI?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Both use the same login CSS, but the admin page has a red heading, the text "Admin Portal Login", a red secure-login button, and an extra client-side check on <code>response.data.isAdmin</code>. A student account that logs in through the admin page receives an access-denied message and its token is not saved by that page.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q41. Where is the admin account created?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">There is a backend script, <code>createAdmin.js</code>, which creates an admin user with <code>isAdmin: true</code> and a bcrypt-hashed password. The credentials are hard-coded in that script for initial setup. For a real deployment they should be moved to environment variables, changed immediately, and the script should not be kept with a real password.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q42. How is a user recognised as an admin after login?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The login response includes <code>isAdmin</code> from the database. The admin page stores <code>isAdmin = "true"</code> in local storage, and <code>AdminRoute</code> checks that value. On the server, admin actions such as viewing purchases or manually unlocking a test check <code>req.user.isAdmin</code> after <code>protect</code> loads the user.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q43. Can a student simply type /admin in the URL?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The client-side <code>AdminRoute</code> will redirect them to <code>/admin-login</code> if the local <code>isAdmin</code> value is not "true". However, client-side checks can be bypassed, so every sensitive admin API must also verify the role on the server. Some admin routes do this; a few read-only admin routes currently do not, which should be fixed.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q44. Why is the admin login page public?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">An unauthenticated person must be able to reach the login form. The page itself is harmless; it only becomes dangerous if the credentials are weak or if admin APIs trust the client. The page does not expose a list of admins or any protected data.</p>
    

  <h3>2.3 Signup and Account Creation</h3>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q45. Explain the signup flow.</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The student opens <code>/signup</code>, enters first name, optional middle name, last name, email, country code and phone number, password and confirm password. The frontend validates the fields and password strength. It sends <code>POST /api/auth/signup</code>. The backend validates names, phone length and email domain, checks for a duplicate email, hashes the password, creates the user with the student role, and returns a JWT so the student is effectively logged in.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q46. Which fields are stored in the User document during signup?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;"><code>firstName</code>, <code>middleName</code> (default empty), <code>lastName</code>, a combined <code>name</code>, <code>email</code>, <code>phone</code> (country code plus number), and the bcrypt <code>password</code> hash. <code>isAdmin</code> defaults to false. Other fields such as <code>college</code>, <code>studentId</code>, <code>readNotes</code>, <code>certificates</code> and <code>performanceStats</code> are filled later or have defaults.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q47. What validations does the backend apply to names?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">First and last names are required and must match <code>/^[a-zA-Z]+(?:\s[a-zA-Z]+)*$/</code>, meaning letters and single spaces only. The middle name is optional but must follow the same rule if provided. This blocks numbers and special characters in names.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q48. How is the phone number validated?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The frontend supplies a country code such as <code>+91</code>. The backend splits the code from the number, looks up the expected digit count in a country map, and checks that the remaining part contains only digits and has exactly that length. For example, <code>+91</code> expects 10 digits. If the country is unknown, the length check is relaxed but the digits-only check remains.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q49. Why does the backend restrict email domains?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It maintains an allow-list of well-known providers such as Gmail, Yahoo, Outlook, Hotmail, iCloud, ProtonMail and Zoho. This catches typos such as "gmial.com", blocks disposable or fake domains, and keeps student contact addresses usable for reset codes. The trade-off is that a student with a legitimate college domain cannot register unless that domain is added to the list.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q50. What are the password rules?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The signup UI enforces at least 8 characters with a letter, a number and a special character, and requires the confirm password to match. The reset-password controller enforces the same strong-password rule on the server. A viva-worthy observation is that the signup endpoint itself does not currently call the strong-password function, so the server should be hardened to match the UI.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q51. What happens if the email is already registered?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The controller queries <code>User.findOne({ email })</code> and returns <code>400</code> with "User already exists". The email field also has a unique index in the schema, which provides a second line of defence against race conditions.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q52. What does signup return to the frontend?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">On success it returns <code>201</code> with the new user's id, name, first name, last name, email, phone and a JWT. It does not explicitly return <code>isAdmin</code>, because a normal signup is never an admin. The frontend can store the token and treat the student as logged in.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q53. Is email verification required before login?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">No. Signup creates an active account immediately and issues a token. There is no verification link or OTP step at registration. The email is only used later for password recovery. Adding email verification would improve trust and prevent fake registrations.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q54. How does the QR code on the home page relate to signup?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The home page uses <code>qrcode.react</code> to generate a QR code for <code>${window.location.origin}/signup?ref=qr</code>. Scanning it opens the normal signup page; the <code>ref=qr</code> query string can be used for attribution. It is a marketing shortcut, not a different authentication method.</p>
    

  <h3>2.4 Forgot Password and Email OTP</h3>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q55. Describe the forgot-password flow.</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The page has four steps: enter email, enter the 6-digit OTP, choose a new password, and see the success state. The backend exposes three endpoints: <code>POST /api/auth/forgot-password</code>, <code>POST /api/auth/verify-reset-otp</code> and <code>POST /api/auth/reset-password</code>. The OTP is emailed if SMTP is configured; in development, if mail delivery fails, the OTP is returned as <code>devOtp</code> for testing.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q56. How is the OTP generated and stored?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The server uses <code>crypto.randomInt(100000, 1000000)</code> to generate a six-digit code. It stores only a bcrypt hash in <code>resetOtpHash</code>, along with <code>resetOtpExpires</code> (10 minutes), <code>resetOtpRequestedAt</code> and an attempt counter. The plain OTP is never saved in the database.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q57. What protections exist around the OTP?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">There is a 60-second resend cooldown, a 10-minute expiry, and a maximum of 5 incorrect attempts. After 5 failures or expiry, the OTP fields are cleared and the student must request a new code. These measures reduce brute-force and spam abuse.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q58. What happens after the OTP is verified?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The server clears the OTP fields, creates a random <code>jti</code>, stores its SHA-256 hash in <code>resetTokenHash</code>, and returns a JWT signed with <code>JWT_SECRET</code> that expires in <strong>10 minutes</strong>. The token payload contains the user id, <code>purpose: "password-reset"</code> and the <code>jti</code>.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q59. Why is a reset token used instead of letting the OTP reset the password directly?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It separates proof of email ownership from the password change, limits the window for the final step, and makes the token one-time. The server checks that the token purpose is correct and that the stored hash of the <code>jti</code> still matches; after a successful reset, the hash is cleared, so replaying the same token fails.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q60. What password rules apply during reset?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;"><code>isStrongPassword</code> requires at least 8 characters, at least one letter, at least one digit and at least one non-alphanumeric character. The new password is hashed with bcrypt before saving, and all reset-related fields are cleared.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q61. How does the system avoid revealing whether an email is registered?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;"><code>forgotPassword</code> always returns the same generic message: "If an account exists for that email, a verification code has been sent." If the email is unknown, it still returns <code>200</code> with that message. This prevents account enumeration.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q62. What happens if SMTP is not configured?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The mail helper reports that delivery failed. The server logs a warning and, when <code>NODE_ENV</code> is not production, includes the OTP in the JSON response as <code>devOtp</code> so the flow can still be demonstrated. In production, no OTP is exposed to the client.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q63. Can the reset flow be used to log in directly?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">No. The reset token has the purpose <code>password-reset</code> and is only accepted by the reset-password endpoint. It is not stored in local storage as a normal session and cannot be used as a Bearer token to call protected APIs.</p>
    

  <h3>2.5 Google, Firebase and Other Access Paths</h3>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q64. Is Google login working in the project?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">No. The project contains <code>src/firebase/firebase.js</code>, which initialises Firebase and exports <code>auth</code> and <code>googleProvider</code>, but the configuration uses placeholder values such as <code>YOUR_API_KEY</code>. No page imports or renders a "Sign in with Google" button, so the flow is scaffolded but not active.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q65. What would be needed to activate Google login?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Create a real Firebase project, replace the placeholder configuration, enable the Google provider in the Firebase console, import <code>signInWithPopup</code> and the provider, add a button, handle the returned Google user, and then either create or link a MongoDB user and issue the application's own JWT. The backend would also need an endpoint to verify the Firebase ID token if server-side trust is required.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q66. Does the QR code count as a login method?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">No. It only opens the signup page with a referral query parameter. The student still creates an account and then logs in with email and password. It is an acquisition feature, not an authentication factor.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q67. Is there any role other than student and admin?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">No. The User schema has one boolean, <code>isAdmin</code>. There is no teacher, moderator or content-editor role. Any future staff role would require either more flags or a proper role field plus server-side checks.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q68. Can a user change their role from the profile page?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">No. <code>updateProfile</code> only allows <code>name</code>, <code>studentId</code> and <code>college</code>. It does not accept <code>isAdmin</code>, password, email or statistics, so a student cannot promote themselves through the profile API.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q69. What happens on logout?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The Navbar removes <code>token</code>, <code>user</code> and <code>isAdmin</code> from local storage and redirects to the home page. Because the token is stored client-side, logout is immediate on that browser, but the old JWT technically remains valid until its 7-day expiry if it was copied elsewhere. A production system would use short-lived access tokens plus refresh-token revocation.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q70. Does the backend support logout or token revocation?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">No. JWTs are stateless and there is no blacklist or session table. Logout only clears the browser. This is acceptable for a college project but is a limitation for a real deployment.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q71. What is the biggest security weakness in the current authentication design?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Storing the JWT in <code>localStorage</code> makes it accessible to any injected script, so XSS becomes token theft. There is also no refresh-token rotation, no email verification, and a few admin read endpoints are not role-protected. The strongest practical improvements are an HTTP-only secure cookie, CSRF protection, rate limiting, email verification and consistent server-side <code>isAdmin</code> middleware.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q72. How would you demonstrate all login paths in a viva?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">First show guest browsing. Then create a student account, log out, log in, open a protected page and show the token in DevTools. Next open the admin login, show that a student account is rejected, then log in with the seeded admin and show the dashboard. Finally run the forgot-password flow and use the development OTP (or the email) to reset the password and log in again.</p>
    

  <div class="tip">
    <strong>Most likely login question:</strong> "How does your login work end to end?"
    Answer in five steps: form submit to <code>/api/auth/login</code>, Mongoose lookup with password selected, bcrypt compare, JWT signed for 7 days, token stored in local storage and sent as a Bearer header to protected APIs.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================ PART 3 ============================ -->

      <div class="page-break"></div>
      <h1 style="color:#0b1f3a;">Part 03 — Page-by-Page Viva Q&amp;A</h1>
      <p style="color:#40525e; font-size:10pt;">Follows the website order. Each page has its route, purpose, main sections and likely questions.</p>
    

<!-- 3.1 HOME -->
<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section first">
  <div class="kicker">Page 3.1</div>
  <h2>Home Page</h2>
  <div class="route" style="font-family:Consolas; font-size:9pt; color:#0f766e;">/ &nbsp;|&nbsp; Public &nbsp;|&nbsp; No API calls</div>

  <p>The landing page is the marketing and entry point of the platform. It introduces Mentor Lab, highlights the four main learning tools, lists the three courses and provides a scannable registration QR code.</p>

  <h3>Sections on the page</h3>
  <table border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="3" width="100%">
    <tbody><tr><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">Section</th><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">What it contains</th></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Hero</td><td valign="top">Background video <code>/frontvideo.mp4</code>, headline "A complete guide for Students", tagline, "Start Practicing" and "Browse Courses" buttons</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Why Choose Us</td><td valign="top">Four clickable cards: Mock Test, Recorded Lectures, Study Notes, Performance Analytics</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Popular Courses</td><td valign="top">Three cards for BCA, BBA and Biotechnology with static mock-test counts and roadmap links</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Scan to Register</td><td valign="top">A generated QR code for the signup page, plus a PNG download button</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Footer</td><td valign="top">Copyright line</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q73. What is the purpose of the home page?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It introduces the platform, directs visitors to the main features and courses, and converts a visitor into a registered student through the QR code and signup call-to-action. It is intentionally public so that a new visitor can explore before creating an account.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q74. What happens when the user clicks "Start Practicing"?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It links to <code>/mock-test</code>. Because that route is wrapped in <code>ProtectedRoute</code>, a guest is redirected to <code>/login</code>, while a logged-in student goes directly to the mock-test portal.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q75. How is the hero video implemented?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">A native HTML5 <code>&lt;video&gt;</code> element uses <code>autoPlay</code>, <code>loop</code>, <code>muted</code> and <code>playsInline</code> with the source <code>/frontvideo.mp4</code> from the public folder. Muting is required by most browsers for autoplay; <code>playsInline</code> prevents full-screen playback on mobile.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q76. What do the four "Why Choose Us" cards do?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">They act as shortcuts: Mock Test opens the test portal, Recorded Lectures opens the lecture page, Study Notes opens the notes hub, and Performance Analytics opens the analytics page. Each card uses CSS custom properties to give itself a different accent colour and glow.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q77. Are the course numbers on the home page dynamic?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">No. Values such as "42 Mock Tests", "34 Mock Tests", "28 Mock Tests" and "6 Months Access" are static marketing text. They are not fetched from the database. A realistic improvement would be to count published tests per course from the backend.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q78. How is the registration QR code generated?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The page imports <code>QRCodeCanvas</code> from <code>qrcode.react</code> and passes <code>value={`${window.location.origin}/signup?ref=qr`}</code>. It uses error-correction level M. The canvas is stored in a React ref so the download button can call <code>toDataURL("image/png")</code> and save the image as <code>mentor-lab-register-qr.png</code>.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q79. Why is window.location.origin used instead of a hard-coded URL?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It makes the QR code point to whichever host is serving the site: localhost during development, a staging domain or the real production domain. This avoids the common mistake of printing a QR code that only works on the developer's machine.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q80. What is the purpose of the #qr-register hash handling?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">If the user arrives at <code>/#qr-register</code>, the <code>useEffect</code> smooth-scrolls to the QR section. This supports a direct "register" link from another page or poster without needing a separate route.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q81. What could be improved on the home page?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Replace static statistics with live counts, add descriptive text to the Why Choose Us icon cards for accessibility and SEO, compress the hero video for mobile data, use local or optimised course images instead of remote Unsplash URLs, and add a visible "Login" call-to-action for returning students.</p>
    
</div>

<!-- 3.2 NAVBAR -->
<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">Page 3.2</div>
  <h2>Navbar / Global Header</h2>
  <div class="route" style="font-family:Consolas; font-size:9pt; color:#0f766e;">Shown on every route &nbsp;|&nbsp; Reads localStorage</div>

  <p>The Navbar is not a route; it is a global component rendered above every page inside the Router. It handles navigation, the course menu, theme switching, authentication state and logout.</p>

  <h3>Sections on the page</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Brand:</strong> Mentor Lab logo and name, linking to the home page.</li>
    <li><strong>Main links:</strong> Home, About, Study Notes, Mock Test, PYQ and other course links.</li>
    <li><strong>Course dropdown:</strong> BCA, BBA and Biotechnology entries with syllabus and roadmap links.</li>
    <li><strong>Auth area:</strong> Login and Signup for guests; Profile, Dashboard (admin), Analytics and Logout for signed-in users.</li>
    <li><strong>Theme control:</strong> toggles the dark/light context.</li>
    <li><strong>Mobile menu:</strong> hamburger button that opens the collapsed navigation.</li>
  </ul>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q82. How does the Navbar know whether a user is logged in?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It reads <code>localStorage.getItem("token")</code> and <code>localStorage.getItem("isAdmin")</code> when it renders. If a token exists, it shows the signed-in menu; if <code>isAdmin === "true"</code>, it also shows the admin dashboard link. It does not call a profile API just to render the header.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q83. How does logout work?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The logout handler removes <code>token</code>, <code>user</code> and <code>isAdmin</code> from local storage. It then redirects to <code>/</code> using <code>window.location.href</code>, which forces a full reload so the Navbar and all route guards immediately see the logged-out state.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q84. Why does the Navbar read local storage instead of React context?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It is a simple approach for a small project, but it means the component only updates when it re-renders, for example after navigation or a reload. A dedicated <code>AuthContext</code> would centralise the user state and update the header instantly after login or logout.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q85. How is the theme toggle connected to the rest of the app?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The Navbar consumes <code>ThemeContext</code>, calls its toggle function, and the provider updates <code>darkMode</code>. <code>App.jsx</code> applies <code>dark-theme</code> or <code>light-theme</code> to the root wrapper, and CSS variables change colours across all pages.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q86. Is the selected theme remembered after a refresh?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">No. <code>ThemeProvider</code> initialises <code>darkMode</code> to <code>true</code> and does not read or write local storage. Adding persistence would require reading a saved value on initial load and saving it inside the toggle function.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q87. How does the course dropdown work?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It is a CSS/React-driven dropdown that reveals links to the BCA, BBA and Biotechnology pages. On mobile it becomes part of the collapsible menu. The links use React Router so navigation does not reload the page.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q88. What is the difference between the Navbar links for guests and students?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Guests see Login and Signup. Signed-in students see Profile, Mock Test, Analytics and Logout. An admin additionally sees the Admin Dashboard link. The change is purely based on local storage flags; the server still enforces access on protected APIs.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q89. What accessibility improvements would you suggest for the Navbar?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Add <code>aria-expanded</code> and <code>aria-controls</code> to the mobile toggle, ensure the dropdown can be operated with a keyboard, add a visible focus style, give icon-only buttons an accessible label, and make sure the contrast ratio is sufficient in both themes.</p>
    
</div>

<!-- 3.3 ABOUT -->
<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">Page 3.3</div>
  <h2>About Us Page</h2>
  <div class="route" style="font-family:Consolas; font-size:9pt; color:#0f766e;">/about &nbsp;|&nbsp; Public &nbsp;|&nbsp; Static content</div>

  <p>The About page explains the platform's mission, offerings, values and team. The content is defined in JavaScript arrays inside the component, so it is easy to update without touching the JSX layout.</p>

  <h3>Sections on the page</h3>
  <table border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="3" width="100%">
    <tbody><tr><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">Section</th><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">What it contains</th></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Hero</td><td valign="top">Badge, headline about learning and growth, short introduction, four statistics</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Mission / Offer / Why</td><td valign="top">Three cards describing the mission, the four offerings and the platform's approach</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Our Values</td><td valign="top">Accessible Education, Expert-Driven Content, Continuous Innovation</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Our Team</td><td valign="top">Six members with photo, name and role</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">CTA</td><td valign="top">"Ready to Start Learning?" with links to home and study notes</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q90. What is the purpose of the About Us page?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It builds trust by explaining who is behind Mentor Lab, what the platform offers and what values guide it. It also gives credit to the six team members and provides a final call-to-action for new learners.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q91. Is the team information fetched from the backend?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">No. It comes from a constant <code>team</code> array in <code>AboutUs.jsx</code>, with fields <code>name</code>, <code>role</code> and <code>photo</code>. Photos are served from the frontend public <code>team</code> folder. Adding a team CMS would make it dynamic.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q92. How are the repeated cards rendered?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The component uses <code>stats.map</code>, <code>values.map</code> and <code>team.map</code>. Each item has a stable key such as <code>label</code>, <code>title</code> or <code>name</code>, which helps React update the list efficiently.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q93. What does the "100% Free Learning Resources" stat mean?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It is a marketing claim shown in the static stats array. In the actual system, notes, PYQs and lectures are free, while some mock tests can be paid. Therefore the statement should be worded carefully or changed to "Free core learning resources" to stay accurate.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q94. Which CSS file styles this page?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;"><code>src/css_files/about.css</code>. The page uses its own classes such as <code>about-hero</code>, <code>about-card</code>, <code>value-card</code>, <code>team-grid</code> and <code>about-cta</code>, in addition to the global theme variables.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q95. What would you improve on the About page?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Replace the emoji icons with accessible SVG icons, add real social or contact links for the team, optimise and standardise team photo sizes, make the stats dynamic if possible, and avoid claiming "100%" free if paid tests exist.</p>
    
</div>

<!-- 3.4 CONTACT -->
<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">Page 3.4</div>
  <h2>Contact Us Page</h2>
  <div class="route" style="font-family:Consolas; font-size:9pt; color:#0f766e;">/contact-us &nbsp;|&nbsp; Public &nbsp;|&nbsp; Client-side form only</div>

  <p>The Contact page provides a simple four-field form and alternative contact details. The form is a controlled React form, but it currently does not send the message to a backend or email service.</p>

  <h3>Sections on the page</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Hero:</strong> "Contact Us" heading and a short invitation.</li>
    <li><strong>Form:</strong> Name, email, subject and message, with a Send Message button.</li>
    <li><strong>Other ways to reach us:</strong> sample email, phone and address.</li>
    <li><strong>Footer:</strong> copyright.</li>
  </ul>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q96. What happens when the contact form is submitted?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The <code>handleSubmit</code> function prevents the default page reload, shows a browser <code>alert</code> thanking the user, and resets the four fields. No HTTP request is made, so the message is not stored or emailed.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q97. Why is this a limitation?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">A real contact form should deliver the message. As written, the user believes the message was sent, but it is lost when the page closes. The project should add a backend endpoint that validates the input and sends mail through Nodemailer, or use a service such as EmailJS/Formspree.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q98. How is the form state managed?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">A single <code>formData</code> object stores name, email, subject and message. <code>handleChange</code> uses the input's <code>name</code> attribute as a computed key: <code>{ ...formData, [e.target.name]: e.target.value }</code>. This avoids four separate <code>useState</code> calls.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q99. What validations are applied?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">All four fields are marked <code>required</code> and the email field uses <code>type="email"</code>, so the browser checks for a basic email pattern. There is no server-side validation, length limit, spam protection or rate limiting because there is no backend submission.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q100. Are the displayed contact details real?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">They are placeholders: <code>support@mentorlab.com</code>, <code>+91-9876543210</code> and "Mentor Lab, Education Hub, India". They should be replaced with verified contact information before publishing.</p>
    
</div>

<!-- 3.5 STUDY NOTES -->
<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">Page 3.5</div>
  <h2>Study Notes Hub</h2>
  <div class="route" style="font-family:Consolas; font-size:9pt; color:#0f766e;">/study-notes &nbsp;|&nbsp; Public &nbsp;|&nbsp; Static cards</div>

  <p>The Study Notes page is a simple course selector. It presents three cards, one for each course, and sends the student to that course's category page.</p>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q101. What is the purpose of the Study Notes page?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It acts as the entry point to the notes library. A student chooses BCA, Biotech or BBA and is taken to the corresponding syllabus/category page, where individual subjects and notes are listed.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q102. Where do the three course links go?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">BCA goes to <code>/bca</code>, Biotech goes to <code>/biotechsylla</code>, and BBA goes to <code>/bba</code>. Each destination uses the same coverflow category layout.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q103. Is any API used on this page?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">No. The three cards and their remote Unsplash images are hard-coded. Notes are fetched later, on the category page, using <code>GET /api/notes?course=...</code>.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q104. What would you improve here?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Add a search box that queries notes across all courses, show a count of available notes per course, use local optimised images, and add a direct "Continue where you left off" section for logged-in students.</p>
    
</div>

<!-- 3.6 BCA -->
<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">Page 3.6</div>
  <h2>BCA Curriculum Page</h2>
  <div class="route" style="font-family:Consolas; font-size:9pt; color:#0f766e;">/bca &nbsp;|&nbsp; Public &nbsp;|&nbsp; Data: bcaSubjects.js</div>

  <p>The BCA page presents the three subject groups as an interactive coverflow carousel. The active card is in the centre, the next card on the right and the previous card on the left.</p>

  <h3>Sections on the page</h3>
  <table border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="3" width="100%">
    <tbody><tr><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">Category</th><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">Subjects</th><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">Examples</th></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Programming Languages</td><td valign="top">7</td><td valign="top">C, C++, Java, Python, HTML &amp; Web Design, JavaScript, CSS</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Logical Subjects</td><td valign="top">7</td><td valign="top">DBMS, DSA, Operating Systems, System Analysis, Computer Architecture, DAA, Networks</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Mathematics Subjects</td><td valign="top">14</td><td valign="top">Calculus, Algebra, Geometry, Probability, Statistics, Correlation, Regression, Sampling</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q105. How does the coverflow carousel work?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The page keeps an <code>index</code> in state. For each category it calculates <code>offset = (i - index + n) % n</code>. Offset 0 gets class <code>cover-center</code>, offset 1 gets <code>cover-right</code>, and all others get <code>cover-left</code>. CSS positions and scales the cards based on those classes. The arrows call <code>go(index +/- 1)</code>, and the modulo expression wraps around at both ends.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q106. Why is the modulo expression written as ((i % n) + n) % n?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">JavaScript's <code>%</code> operator can return a negative result for negative inputs. Adding <code>n</code> before the second modulo keeps the result in the range <code>0..n-1</code>, so clicking "previous" on the first card wraps to the last card correctly.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q107. Where does the category data come from?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;"><code>src/data/bcaSubjects.js</code> exports <code>bcaCategories</code> and a helper <code>getCategoryBySlug</code>. Each category has a slug, icon, name, description, image URL and an <code>items</code> array of subject names.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q108. How does the user open a category?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Each card is a React Router <code>Link</code> to <code>/bca/${c.slug}</code>. Clicking any card, even a side card, opens that category. The route is handled by <code>BCACategoryPage</code>.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q109. What does the "Subjects" count show?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It displays <code>c.items.length</code>, so it is computed from the actual data rather than hard-coded. Adding a subject to the array automatically updates the badge.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q110. Does the BCA page fetch anything from the server?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">No. It only loads the static category data and remote images. The note API is called on the category detail page, not here.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q111. What are the responsive and accessibility concerns of the carousel?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">On small screens the three-card layout can overflow; CSS must reduce card size or switch to a simple list. The arrow buttons have <code>aria-label</code> values, which is good, but keyboard users should also be able to focus and activate cards, and the animated transform should respect <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>.</p>
    
</div>

<!-- 3.7 BCA CATEGORY -->
<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">Page 3.7</div>
  <h2>BCA Category Page</h2>
  <div class="route" style="font-family:Consolas; font-size:9pt; color:#0f766e;">/bca/:categorySlug &nbsp;|&nbsp; Public &nbsp;|&nbsp; GET /api/notes?course=BCA</div>

  <p>This page lists every subject in the selected category, matches each subject to a note in the database, and shows Read Online, PDF or "Coming Soon". For the Programming Languages category it also renders the online Code Compiler.</p>

  <h3>Sections on the page</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Header:</strong> Back link, category name and description.</li>
    <li><strong>Detail panel:</strong> icon, category title, subject count and a short hint.</li>
    <li><strong>Subject list:</strong> each subject with a dot, name, note actions or a Coming Soon badge.</li>
    <li><strong>Code Compiler:</strong> only when <code>categorySlug === "programming-languages"</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q112. How is the category selected from the URL?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;"><code>useParams()</code> reads <code>categorySlug</code>. The helper <code>getCategoryBySlug</code> searches the static BCA data. If no category matches, the page shows a "Category not found" message and a back link instead of crashing.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q113. Which API does this page call?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;"><code>GET /api/notes?course=BCA</code>. The backend filters notes by the exact course string and excludes the large <code>content</code> field from the list response, returning lightweight metadata such as title, subject, <code>fileId</code>, <code>pdfUrl</code> and <code>hasContent</code>.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q114. How is a note matched to a subject?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;"><code>getNoteForSubject</code> lowercases both the subject name from the static array and <code>note.subject</code>, then looks for an exact match. This means the admin must type the subject name exactly as it appears in the curriculum data; otherwise the page shows Coming Soon.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q115. What is the difference between Read Online and PDF?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Read Online links to <code>/notes/:id</code>, which renders the extracted text as a styled web page. PDF links directly to <code>/api/notes/:id/pdf</code>, which streams the original file from GridFS with an inline content disposition. Read Online appears if the note has a file, a URL or web content; PDF appears only if a file or URL exists.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q116. Why does the code compiler appear only on Programming Languages?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The compiler is useful for C, C++, Java, Python, JavaScript and HTML, which are all in that category. Showing it on Mathematics or Logical Subjects would add unnecessary weight and confuse the page's purpose.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q117. How does the page handle a subject with no note?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It renders a <code>soon-badge</code> with the text "Coming Soon". This is a graceful empty state: the curriculum remains visible, and the absence of content is communicated without an error.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q118. What happens if the notes API fails?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The <code>.catch</code> logs the error to the console and leaves <code>notes</code> as an empty array. Every subject then shows Coming Soon. A better UX would show an inline error message and a retry button, rather than silently implying that no notes exist.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q119. What are the limitations of the exact subject match?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Small differences in spelling, punctuation or spacing break the match. For example, "C++ Programming" and "C++" are different strings. A normalised match or a <code>subjectId</code> foreign key would be more reliable than free-text matching.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q120. How would you add a new BCA subject?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Add its name to the correct category's <code>items</code> array in <code>bcaSubjects.js</code>. Then upload a note through the admin dashboard with <code>course = BCA</code> and a <code>subject</code> string that exactly matches the new item. The page will automatically show Read Online/PDF.</p>
    
</div>

<!-- 3.8 BBA -->
<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">Page 3.8</div>
  <h2>BBA Curriculum Page</h2>
  <div class="route" style="font-family:Consolas; font-size:9pt; color:#0f766e;">/bba &nbsp;|&nbsp; Public &nbsp;|&nbsp; Data: bbaCategories.js</div>

  <p>The BBA page uses the same coverflow pattern as BCA but with six business categories. It reuses <code>bca.css</code> and adds a BBA background class.</p>

  <h3>Sections on the page</h3>
  <table border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="3" width="100%">
    <tbody><tr><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">Category</th><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">Focus</th></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Management &amp; Strategy</td><td valign="top">Management principles, strategy and organisational behaviour</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Finance &amp; Accounts</td><td valign="top">Accounting, financial management and cost concepts</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Analytics &amp; Tech</td><td valign="top">Business analytics, MIS and data-driven decisions</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Marketing Dynamics</td><td valign="top">Marketing management, research and consumer behaviour</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Environment &amp; Law</td><td valign="top">Business law, ethics and the business environment</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Operations &amp; Practice</td><td valign="top">Operations, supply chain and practical business projects</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q121. How is BBA different from BCA technically?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The implementation is almost identical: the same carousel logic, the same CSS file and the same route pattern. The differences are the data source (<code>bbaCategories</code>), the six categories instead of three, the heading text and the <code>bba-bg</code> class.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q122. Why does this page reuse bca.css?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The card, coverflow and detail-panel styles are shared. Reusing the stylesheet keeps the visual language consistent and avoids duplicating CSS. A future refactor could rename it to something neutral such as <code>curriculum.css</code>.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q123. How many categories and subjects does BBA have?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">There are six categories, and each category's subject count is computed from its <code>items</code> array rather than hard-coded. The count badge updates automatically if the data changes.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q124. What happens when a BBA card is clicked?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It navigates to <code>/bba/:categorySlug</code>, which is rendered by <code>BbaCategoryPage</code>. That page validates the slug and lists the subjects with the same note-matching pattern as BCA.</p>
    
</div>

<!-- 3.9 BBA CATEGORY -->
<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">Page 3.9</div>
  <h2>BBA Category Page</h2>
  <div class="route" style="font-family:Consolas; font-size:9pt; color:#0f766e;">/bba/:categorySlug &nbsp;|&nbsp; Public &nbsp;|&nbsp; GET /api/notes?course=BBA</div>

  <p>This page lists the subjects of a chosen BBA category, matches notes and offers Read Online/PDF links. It does not render the code compiler.</p>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q125. How does the BBA category page differ from the BCA category page?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It uses <code>bbaCategories</code> and queries <code>GET /api/notes?course=BBA</code>. It does not import <code>CodeCompiler</code>. The rest of the structure, the exact subject matching and the Coming Soon fallback are the same.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q126. How does it handle an invalid slug?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">If <code>getCategoryBySlug</code> returns nothing, it displays "Category not found" with a link back to <code>/bba</code>. This prevents a blank page or a runtime error when a user edits the URL.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q127. Which notes appear on this page?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Only notes whose <code>course</code> field exactly equals <code>BBA</code> and whose <code>subject</code> exactly matches the subject name (case-insensitive). The list API deliberately excludes full content to keep the response small.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q128. What is a possible improvement for all three category pages?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Extract the shared logic into one reusable <code>CourseCategoryPage</code> component that receives the course name, data source and an optional compiler flag. This would remove three near-duplicate files and reduce maintenance effort.</p>
    
</div>

<!-- 3.10 BIOTECH SYLLABUS -->
<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">Page 3.10</div>
  <h2>Biotechnology Syllabus Page</h2>
  <div class="route" style="font-family:Consolas; font-size:9pt; color:#0f766e;">/biotechsylla &nbsp;|&nbsp; Public &nbsp;|&nbsp; Data: biotechCategories.js</div>

  <p>This is the Biotechnology equivalent of the BCA/BBA curriculum pages. It has three categories and a Biotech-specific background class.</p>

  <h3>Sections on the page</h3>
  <table border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="3" width="100%">
    <tbody><tr><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">Category</th><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">Coverage</th></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Foundation Sciences</td><td valign="top">Cell biology, chemistry, biostatistics, biomolecules, lab techniques</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Core Biotechnology</td><td valign="top">Biochemistry, microbiology, molecular biology, enzymes, rDNA technology</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Advanced &amp; Applied</td><td valign="top">Genetic engineering, immunology, bioprocess, genomics, fermentation</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q129. What is the purpose of this page?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It organises the Biotechnology syllabus into three progressive categories and lets the student open a category to see its subjects, notes and PDFs. It is separate from the Biotech roadmap, which shows a six-phase career/learning path.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q130. How does the page reuse existing code?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It follows the same carousel implementation as BCA and BBA, imports <code>bca.css</code>, and adds <code>biotech-bg</code> for the background. The data comes from <code>biotechCategories.js</code>. This consistency keeps the UI familiar.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q131. What is the difference between the syllabus page and the Biotech roadmap?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The syllabus page is subject-oriented and links to study material. The roadmap at <code>/biotech</code> is a six-phase visual guide from foundation sciences to biopharmaceutical development, intended for planning and motivation rather than note navigation.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q132. Where would a new Biotech category be added?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Add an object with <code>slug</code>, <code>icon</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>desc</code>, <code>image</code> and <code>items</code> to <code>biotechCategories.js</code>. The carousel and the route will pick it up automatically because the page maps over the array.</p>
    
</div>

<!-- 3.11 BIOTECH CATEGORY -->
<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">Page 3.11</div>
  <h2>Biotech Category Page</h2>
  <div class="route" style="font-family:Consolas; font-size:9pt; color:#0f766e;">/biotechsylla/:categorySlug &nbsp;|&nbsp; Public &nbsp;|&nbsp; GET /api/notes?course=BIOTECH</div>

  <p>This page lists the subjects of a selected Biotechnology category and matches them to notes stored with <code>course = BIOTECH</code>.</p>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q133. Which course value does the note API receive?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It queries <code>/api/notes?course=BIOTECH</code>. The backend performs an exact match on the <code>course</code> field, so notes uploaded as "Biotechnology" instead of "BIOTECH" will not appear. The admin upload form should enforce the correct course value.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q134. How are note links shown?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">If a matching note has a PDF or URL, the page shows Read Online and PDF. If it only has web content, it shows Read Online. If there is no matching note, it shows Coming Soon. This is the same logic as the other two category pages.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q135. Why does this page not have a code compiler?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Biotechnology is not a programming course, so a compiler would not be relevant. The compiler is conditionally rendered only for BCA's Programming Languages category.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q136. What is a data-consistency risk here?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The subject names in the static data and the subject names stored with uploaded notes must match exactly. A mismatch produces a false "Coming Soon". Using IDs or a normalised lookup would eliminate this risk.</p>
    
</div>

<!-- 3.12 ROADMAPS -->
<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">Pages 3.12 - 3.14</div>
  <h2>BCA, BBA and Biotech Roadmaps</h2>
  <div class="route" style="font-family:Consolas; font-size:9pt; color:#0f766e;">/roadmapbca &nbsp; /bbaroadmap &nbsp; /biotech &nbsp;|&nbsp; Public &nbsp;|&nbsp; Static</div>

  <p>Each course has a six-phase roadmap. The pages are static, visually rich and designed to show a student what to learn and when. They are separate from the syllabus pages, which are used to open notes.</p>

  <h3>Sections on each page</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Header:</strong> a step-by-step guide title for the course.</li>
    <li><strong>Phases 1-3:</strong> foundation, core subjects and intermediate/web/advanced skills.</li>
    <li><strong>Phase 4:</strong> specialisation or electives.</li>
    <li><strong>Phase 5:</strong> projects, internship and practical experience.</li>
    <li><strong>Phase 6:</strong> career, placement and alumni/graduation.</li>
    <li><strong>CTA:</strong> a note linking back to the course's study notes.</li>
  </ul>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q137. What is the purpose of a roadmap page?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It gives students a structured, phase-wise plan rather than a flat subject list. It answers "what should I learn next?" and connects academic subjects with projects, specialisation and placement preparation.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q138. How are the roadmap cards built?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">They are static JSX sections. Each card has a phase tag, a focus sentence and a <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> of subjects. CSS classes such as <code>card-blue</code>, <code>card-green</code> and <code>card-purple</code> give each card a different colour.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q139. How many phases does each roadmap have?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">All three have six phases. BCA moves from programming foundations to core computing, web/networks, AI specialisation, build/deploy and career. BBA moves from business foundations to core management, intermediate subjects, specialisation, practical experience and graduation. Biotech moves from foundational sciences to core biotech, advanced technical skills, bioinformatics, stem cells and biopharmaceutical development.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q140. Why is Phase 4 called the elective phase?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It lets students choose a direction. BCA lists AI/ML, cyber security, cloud, mobile, data science and IoT; BBA lists finance, marketing, HR, entrepreneurship, analytics and supply chain. This mirrors how many degree programmes offer specialisation tracks.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q141. Is the roadmap data stored in the database?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">No. It is hard-coded in <code>roadmapbca.jsx</code>, <code>bbaroadmap.jsx</code> and <code>biotech.jsx</code>. This is fine for a fixed curriculum, but editing requires a code change and redeploy. A CMS or MongoDB collection would make it admin-editable.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q142. What is the "Explore Study Notes" button for?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It links the roadmap back to the corresponding syllabus page: BCA to <code>/bca</code>, BBA to <code>/bba</code> and Biotech to <code>/biotechsylla</code>. This closes the loop between planning and actual study material.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q143. Why does the project contain both roadmapbca.jsx and BCARoadmap.jsx?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;"><code>App.jsx</code> imports and routes <code>roadmapbca.jsx</code>. <code>BCARoadmap.jsx</code> is an unused duplicate of the same content. It should be removed to avoid confusion and dead code. This is a useful point to mention when asked about code quality.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q144. Are the roadmaps responsive?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">They use CSS grid classes and a row divider. The layout can stack on smaller screens, but each roadmap has its own CSS file, so responsiveness must be checked separately for <code>roadmapbca.css</code>, <code>roadmapbb.css</code> and <code>bio.css</code>.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q145. What improvement would make the roadmaps more useful?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Add progress tracking: let a student tick off phases, store the progress in the User document, and show a completion percentage. Linking each subject to its note or mock test would turn the roadmap into an actionable learning path instead of a static poster.</p>
    
</div>

<!-- 3.13 NOTE VIEWER -->
<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">Page 3.15</div>
  <h2>Note Viewer</h2>
  <div class="route" style="font-family:Consolas; font-size:9pt; color:#0f766e;">/notes/:id &nbsp;|&nbsp; Public &nbsp;|&nbsp; GET /api/notes/:id</div>

  <p>The Note Viewer turns a PDF's extracted text into a clean reading page. It adds a table of contents, a reading-progress bar, reading-time metadata, language detection and a link to the original PDF.</p>

  <h3>Sections on the page</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Reading progress bar:</strong> fixed at the top, based on scroll position.</li>
    <li><strong>Article header:</strong> back link, course badge, detected language, title, subject, description, word count and reading time.</li>
    <li><strong>Content area:</strong> text converted into paragraphs, lists and headings.</li>
    <li><strong>Table of contents:</strong> right-side unit list with active-section highlighting.</li>
    <li><strong>Footer:</strong> PDF download link when the original file exists.</li>
  </ul>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q146. How does the page load a note?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It reads the <code>id</code> from <code>useParams()</code> and calls <code>GET /api/notes/:id</code>. While waiting it shows "Loading note"; on failure it shows a "Note not found" panel with a link back to the notes hub.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q147. How is the note content rendered?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The backend stores extracted PDF text in the <code>content</code> field. The frontend passes it to <code>renderPlainText</code> in <code>utils/markdown.js</code>, which converts blank lines into paragraphs, bullet characters into lists, and heading-like lines into <code>h2</code>/<code>h3</code> elements. The result is inserted with <code>dangerouslySetInnerHTML</code>, but the renderer calls <code>escapeHtml</code> on every text fragment first, so stored text cannot inject raw HTML.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q148. Is the content actually Markdown?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">No. Despite the file name <code>markdown.js</code>, it is a plain-text-to-HTML formatter with heuristics. It detects headings using patterns such as "Unit 1", "Chapter 2", numbered titles and all-uppercase lines. It does not support Markdown syntax like <code>**bold**</code> or links.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q149. How is the table of contents generated?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;"><code>extractHeadings</code> runs the same heading-detection logic and returns a list of <code>{ level, text }</code>. The TOC renders level-2 entries as units and level-3 entries as sub-items. Each heading receives an <code>id="nv-sec-i"</code>, and clicking a TOC item calls <code>scrollIntoView</code>.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q150. How does the page highlight the current section?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">A scroll listener computes total progress and loops through the heading elements. The last heading whose top is above 120 pixels becomes the active index. The TOC then gives that item an active class and scrolls the TOC list so the active item stays centred.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q151. How is reading time calculated?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It counts words by splitting the content on whitespace and divides by 200 words per minute, with a minimum of one minute. The word count is also displayed as a metadata chip.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q152. How does language detection work?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;"><code>detectLanguage</code> applies regular expressions to the content. <code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;</code> or <code>std::cout</code> identifies C++; <code>public class</code> or <code>System.out.</code> identifies Java; <code>def</code>/<code>import</code> identifies Python; <code>#include &lt;</code> or <code>printf</code> identifies C; <code>const</code>/<code>useState</code> identifies JavaScript; HTML tags identify Web. If nothing matches, no language badge is shown.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q153. Does opening a note mark it as read?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Yes, automatically. If a token exists, the page calls <code>POST /api/certificate/notes/:id/read</code>. The backend adds the note ID to the user's <code>readNotes</code> array using <code>$addToSet</code>, so duplicate reads do not create duplicates. The call is fire-and-forget; a failure does not block reading.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q154. How does the "Back" link know where to go?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">A <code>COURSE_ROUTES</code> map converts the note's course to <code>/bca</code>, <code>/bba</code> or <code>/biotechsylla</code>. Unknown courses fall back to <code>/study-notes</code>. This keeps the navigation contextual.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q155. How is the PDF download served?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The link points to <code>/api/notes/:id/pdf</code>. The backend streams the GridFS file with the correct content type. In <code>index.js</code>, PDFs served from <code>/uploads</code> are set to <code>Content-Disposition: inline</code>; the notes route also streams inline, so the browser can preview the PDF in a new tab.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q156. What are the limitations of this page?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Heading detection is heuristic and can misclassify lines; long PDFs may produce a noisy TOC. The page marks a note as read immediately on open, even if the student does not scroll. There is no bookmark, highlight, font-size control or offline support. The content is escaped, which is safe, but it also means intentionally formatted HTML in a note will display as text.</p>
    
</div>

<!-- 3.14 RECORDED LECTURES -->
<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">Page 3.14</div>
  <h2>Recorded Lectures</h2>
  <div class="route" style="font-family:Consolas; font-size:9pt; color:#0f766e;">/recorded-lectures &nbsp;|&nbsp; Public &nbsp;|&nbsp; Static playlist</div>

  <p>The page is a simple video-learning screen with one main player and a static playlist sidebar.</p>

  <h3>Sections on the page</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Header:</strong> "Recorded Lectures" and the line "Watch and revise at your own pace."</li>
    <li><strong>Main player:</strong> HTML5 video with the file <code>/intro2.mp4</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Lecture information:</strong> instructor, duration and upload date.</li>
    <li><strong>Course Playlist:</strong> four HTML/CSS lectures with titles and durations.</li>
    <li><strong>Footer:</strong> copyright line.</li>
  </ul>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q157. Is the recorded-lecture content dynamic?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">No. The single MP4 and all four playlist entries are hard-coded JSX. The playlist items are not clickable and do not change the video source. There is no lecture API, no progress tracking and no database model used on this page, even though the backend has an unused <code>Lecture</code> model.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q158. How is the video played?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It uses the browser's native <code>&lt;video controls&gt;</code> element with a single MP4 source from the public folder. The browser handles play, pause, seeking, volume and full screen; no custom video library is required.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q159. What is inconsistent on this page?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The footer says "Drona Gyan Platform", while the rest of the site uses "Mentor Lab", and the <code>package.json</code> name is <code>drona-gyan</code>. This suggests the project was renamed. The brand should be unified before a demo.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q160. How would you make the lecture page production-ready?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Store lectures in MongoDB or a video platform, make the playlist update the player, add course and chapter filters, track watched progress for signed-in students, support captions and playback speed, and move large videos to a CDN or object storage rather than the frontend public folder.</p>
    
</div>

<!-- 3.15 PYQ -->
<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">Page 3.15</div>
  <h2>PYQ Portal</h2>
  <div class="route" style="font-family:Consolas; font-size:9pt; color:#0f766e;">/pyq &nbsp;|&nbsp; Public &nbsp;|&nbsp; GET /api/pyq?universityId=...</div>

  <p>The Previous Year Questions portal lets a student choose a university, then browse a course-wise list of subjects and download the uploaded PDF for each. It is a two-screen experience: university carousel and subject archive.</p>

  <h3>Sections on the page</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>University carousel:</strong> six universities with image, city, category, description and a "View PYQs" button.</li>
    <li><strong>Auto-advance:</strong> the carousel moves automatically when not hovered or animating.</li>
    <li><strong>University screen:</strong> back button, heading, and three course columns (Biotech, BCA, BBA).</li>
    <li><strong>Subject list:</strong> each subject with a View/Download button if a PDF exists, or "No PDF uploaded".</li>
  </ul>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q161. Which universities are included?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Ranchi University, Vinoba Bhave University (Hazaribagh), Dr. Shyama Prasad Mukherjee University (Ranchi), Binod Bihari Mahto Koyalanchal University (Dhanbad), BIT Mesra (Ranchi) and Sidhu Kanhu Murmu University (Dumka). Their IDs are <code>ranchi</code>, <code>vbu</code>, <code>dspmu</code>, <code>bbmku</code>, <code>bit</code> and <code>skmu</code>.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q162. How does the university carousel work?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It keeps an <code>index</code>, calculates how many cards fit the viewport, and translates the card track with a CSS transform. It supports previous/next arrows, dot navigation, swipe gestures, hover pause and a timed auto-advance. Side cards get fade/scale classes for depth.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q163. What happens after selecting a university?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;"><code>selectedUni</code> is set, the carousel view is replaced by the subject archive, and <code>fetchPYQs(universityId)</code> calls <code>GET /api/pyq?universityId=...</code>. The response is converted into a lookup map keyed by normalised course and subject.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q164. How does the page match a PYQ to a subject?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;"><code>normalizeKey</code> lowercases the text, collapses whitespace and trims it. The map key is <code>course:subject</code>. When rendering a subject, the page builds the same key and looks up the PDF. This makes matching case- and spacing-insensitive, but punctuation and spelling still matter.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q165. Where does the subject list come from?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It is a static <code>subjectsData</code> object with long arrays for <code>bca</code>, <code>bba</code> and <code>biotech</code>. The API only supplies the uploaded PDF records; it does not define the curriculum on this page.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q166. How is a PYQ PDF opened?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The link points to <code>/api/pyq/:id/pdf</code>. The backend finds the record, opens a GridFS download stream from the <code>pyqs</code> bucket, sets the content type to PDF and streams it inline. If the file is missing, it returns an error status.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q167. What happens for a subject with no uploaded paper?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The page shows a disabled "No PDF uploaded" state instead of a link. This is a clear empty state, although it can look like a large amount of missing content if the archive is new.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q168. How are PYQs uploaded?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">An admin uses the dashboard's Upload PYQ section, choosing a university, course, subject and PDF. The form posts to <code>POST /api/pyq/upload</code> as multipart form data. Multer keeps the file in memory, and the backend writes it to the <code>pyqs</code> GridFS bucket with metadata.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q169. What are the limitations of the PYQ portal?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The university and subject lists are static, so adding a university requires a code change. Matching is by text rather than IDs. There is no search, no year/semester filter, no download count and no preview thumbnail. The upload route checks for a valid token but not specifically for an admin role.</p>
    
</div>

<!-- 3.16 MOCK TEST -->
<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">Page 3.16</div>
  <h2>Mock Test Portal</h2>
  <div class="route" style="font-family:Consolas; font-size:9pt; color:#0f766e;">/mock-test &nbsp;|&nbsp; Protected &nbsp;|&nbsp; /api/mocktest/*</div>

  <p>The mock-test module is the most interactive part of the project. It includes test selection, paid-test unlocking, a timed exam interface, a question palette, server-side scoring, a result scorecard and certificate checking.</p>

  <h3>Screens and sections</h3>
  <table border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="3" width="100%">
    <tbody><tr><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">Screen</th><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">Main elements</th></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Selection</td><td valign="top">Search box, test cards, subject/topic tags, question count, duration, price badge, start button</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Payment modal</td><td valign="top">Amount, UPI ID, QR image, UTR input, Razorpay checkout or simulated confirmation</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Exam</td><td valign="top">Full-screen layout, timer, question, four options, Save &amp; Next, Mark for Review, Clear, palette, Submit</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Result</td><td valign="top">Scorecard, attempted/correct/wrong counts, final score, review button, restart, certificate prompt</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q170. How does a student start a mock test?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The page calls <code>GET /api/mocktest</code> with the Bearer token. The backend hides <code>correctAnswer</code> from the list and marks each test with <code>purchased</code> or <code>paymentPending</code>. Clicking Start calls <code>openTest(test)</code>. If the test is free or already purchased, the exam screen opens immediately; otherwise the payment modal opens.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q171. How is access to a paid test enforced?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The backend helper <code>hasAccess(userId, test)</code> returns true when the price is zero or when a Purchase document exists for that user and test. It is checked before fetching the full test, before submitting, and inside the order flow. The unique index on <code>userId + testId</code> prevents duplicate purchase records.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q172. Which payment paths are supported?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Three paths: free tests, real Razorpay checkout when valid keys are configured, and a manual UPI fallback when Razorpay is not configured. The fallback shows the admin's UPI ID/QR, asks for the transaction reference (UTR), and calls <code>simulate-pay</code>. The purchase is stored with status <code>simulated</code>.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q173. How is a real Razorpay payment verified?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The backend creates an order with amount <code>price * 100</code> (paise). After checkout, the frontend sends <code>orderId</code>, <code>paymentId</code> and <code>signature</code> to <code>POST /api/mocktest/:id/verify</code>. The server recomputes an HMAC-SHA256 of <code>orderId|paymentId</code> with the Razorpay secret and compares it to the signature. Only a match creates a <code>paid</code> purchase.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q174. What is the purpose of the UTR field?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">In the manual UPI flow, the student pays outside the app and enters the UPI transaction reference. The server requires a non-empty reference for a paid test and stores it as <code>paymentId</code>. This creates an audit trail for the admin, although the server cannot automatically prove that the UTR is genuine.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q175. How does the exam timer work?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">When a test starts, <code>timeLeft</code> is set to <code>(test.duration || 60) * 60</code> seconds. A <code>setInterval</code> decrements it every second while the test screen is active. When it reaches zero, <code>handleSubmit(true)</code> is called automatically. The final submit always happens on the server.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q176. What do the question-palette colours mean?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Green means answered, red means not answered, yellow means marked for review. The palette also highlights the current question. The student can jump to any question by clicking its number, and the status of the previous question is updated when moving away.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q177. What is the difference between "Save &amp; Next", "Mark for Review" and "Clear"?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Save &amp; Next records the selected option and advances. Mark for Review keeps the question flagged for a later check. Clear removes the selected answer and marks the question as not answered. These actions only change local React state until the final submit.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q178. How is the test scored?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">On the server, each attempted question is compared with its stored correct answer. The score is <code>correct * 4 - wrong * 1</code>, and the maximum is <code>questions.length * 4</code>. Unattempted questions add zero. There is no floor, so a test with many wrong answers can produce a negative score.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q179. Why is scoring done on the server instead of the browser?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Server-side scoring prevents a student from editing JavaScript to inflate the result or reading the answer key from the page. The list endpoint removes <code>correctAnswer</code>, and the submit endpoint recomputes the score from the database copy of the test.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q180. What happens after a test is submitted?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The server creates a <code>Result</code> document with the answers, counts and score, and returns <code>correct</code>, <code>wrong</code>, <code>score</code>, <code>totalQuestions</code>, <code>maxScore</code> and <code>resultId</code>. The frontend also posts to <code>/api/auth/add-activity</code> to add a recent-activity entry and increment the tests-taken count. It then asks the certificate endpoint whether the course is complete.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q181. How does the result screen show the certificate?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">After submission, the page calls <code>GET /api/certificate/progress/:subject</code> using the test's subject. If the course is complete and a certificate exists or was just issued, the result screen shows a "Congratulations - Course Completed!" card with a link to the certificate.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q182. How does a student review answers?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The result screen has a Review button that navigates to <code>/test-review/:resultId</code>. The review page fetches the saved result and the associated questions, then shows the selected answer, the correct answer and the status for each question.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q183. What happens if the student refreshes during a test?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The exam state is held in React state and is not persisted. A refresh returns the student to the selection screen and loses the in-progress answers. A production exam system would save answers to the server or local storage periodically.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q184. What are the main limitations of the mock-test module?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">There is no negative-score floor, no section-wise timing, no randomised question order, no attempt limit, no server-side autosave and no anti-cheating/tab-switch detection. The manual UPI path trusts the entered UTR. The result review shows a small icon bug: a correct selected answer also displays a cross icon because the "Your Answer" label uses a cross for both cases.</p>
    
</div>

<!-- 3.17 TEST REVIEW -->
<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">Page 3.17</div>
  <h2>Test Review Page</h2>
  <div class="route" style="font-family:Consolas; font-size:9pt; color:#0f766e;">/test-review/:resultId &nbsp;|&nbsp; Protected &nbsp;|&nbsp; GET /api/mocktest/result/:resultId</div>

  <p>The review page is a detailed answer sheet for one completed test. It is reached from the result screen or from a Profile activity item.</p>

  <h3>Sections on the page</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Header:</strong> back button, "Answer Review: [test title]" and a divider.</li>
    <li><strong>Summary row:</strong> score, attempted, correct, wrong and unattempted.</li>
    <li><strong>Question cards:</strong> each question, all options, correct answer highlighting, selected answer highlighting and a status badge.</li>
    <li><strong>Footer action:</strong> "Back to Mock Tests".</li>
  </ul>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q185. Which API does the review page use?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;"><code>GET /api/mocktest/result/:resultId</code> with the Bearer token. The backend finds the result by ID and also filters by <code>userId: req.user._id</code>, so one student cannot open another student's result by guessing the ID.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q186. How does it combine answers with questions?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The response contains the saved <code>result</code> and the current <code>questions</code> from the test. For each question, the page finds the matching entry in <code>result.answers</code> by <code>questionIndex</code>, then uses its <code>selectedOption</code> and <code>isCorrect</code> values to style the options.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q187. How are the three states displayed?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">A question is <strong>correct</strong> if a selected option matches the correct answer, <strong>wrong</strong> if an option was selected but does not match, and <strong>unattempted</strong> if <code>selectedOption</code> is -1 or missing. The card border and badge colour change for each state.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q188. What happens if the result does not exist?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The page catches the error and shows the server message with a "Go Back" button. It does not crash or show an empty review card.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q189. What small bug is visible in the review UI?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">When the selected option is also the correct option, the code adds both "Correct Answer" and a cross-marked "Your Answer" tag. The second tag should use a check mark or a neutral "Your Answer" label. It is a presentation bug, not a scoring bug.</p>
    
</div>

<!-- 3.18 ANALYTICS -->
<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">Page 3.18</div>
  <h2>Performance Analytics</h2>
  <div class="route" style="font-family:Consolas; font-size:9pt; color:#0f766e;">/analytics &nbsp;|&nbsp; Protected &nbsp;|&nbsp; GET /api/auth/profile</div>

  <p>The analytics page turns the student's stored activity into visual feedback. It has four tabs: Overview, Score Trends, Subject Analysis and Test History.</p>

  <h3>Sections on the page</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Overview:</strong> six stat cards (Tests Taken, Accuracy, Rank, Day Streak, Hours Learnt, Avg Score), recent score trend and subject performance.</li>
    <li><strong>Score Trends:</strong> score progression chart for the last 10 tests.</li>
    <li><strong>Subject Analysis:</strong> per-course performance and monthly activity.</li>
    <li><strong>Test History:</strong> the full recent-activity list with scores and review links.</li>
    <li><strong>Insights:</strong> latest trend and a recommendation based on the data.</li>
  </ul>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q190. Where does the analytics data come from?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It comes from <code>GET /api/auth/profile</code>, specifically the <code>performanceStats</code> object and the <code>recentActivity</code> array. There is no separate analytics API; the calculations are done in the React component.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q191. How is accuracy calculated?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Each activity has a score string such as "24 / 100". <code>parseScore</code> splits it into obtained and total marks. <code>calculateAccuracy</code> sums all obtained marks and all maximum marks, then returns <code>obtained / total * 100</code>, rounded to a whole number.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q192. How is the score trend built?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It takes the last 10 activities, converts each score to a percentage and returns a labelled bar for each test. The chart is CSS-based rather than a chart library: each bar's height is set to the percentage value.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q193. How is subject performance inferred?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It inspects the activity title for the words "BCA", "BBA" or "biotech" and groups the scores into those buckets. If none match, the activity goes into "General". This is a heuristic, not a real subject field.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q194. How is the day streak calculated?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It builds a set of unique activity dates, sorts them newest first, and walks backwards from today. Each date within one day of the previous date increases the streak; a gap stops the count. Multiple activities on the same day count once.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q195. Which stat cards may not update as expected?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;"><code>addActivity</code> increments only <code>performanceStats.testsTaken</code>. It does not update <code>avgScore</code>, <code>rank</code> or <code>hoursLearnt</code>, so those cards can remain at their defaults even after several tests. Accuracy, streak and trends are computed from activities and do update.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q196. What are the limitations of the analytics page?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It relies on activity titles for subject classification, uses a score string instead of numeric fields, has no date-range filter, and does not compare the student with other students for a real rank. A dedicated analytics endpoint with aggregated result data would be more reliable.</p>
    
</div>

<!-- 3.19 PROFILE -->
<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">Page 3.19</div>
  <h2>Student Profile</h2>
  <div class="route" style="font-family:Consolas; font-size:9pt; color:#0f766e;">/profile &nbsp;|&nbsp; Logged-in student &nbsp;|&nbsp; GET/PUT /api/auth/profile</div>

  <p>The profile page shows identity, college information, performance statistics, certificate count and recent test activity. It also allows the student to edit the college name.</p>

  <h3>Sections on the page</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Header:</strong> initials avatar, role badge, full name, email, student ID and college.</li>
    <li><strong>College editor:</strong> add or edit the college, with save/cancel and a success message.</li>
    <li><strong>Sidebar:</strong> performance overview and earned-certificate count.</li>
    <li><strong>Main panel:</strong> recent test activity with score and Review button.</li>
  </ul>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q197. How does the page load data?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Two requests run in parallel: <code>GET /api/auth/profile</code> for the user document and <code>GET /api/certificate/mine</code> for the certificate list. The certificate count is the length of the returned array. If the profile fails, the page shows an error; if certificates fail, the count simply becomes zero.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q198. What can the student edit?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Only the college name. The save handler sends <code>PUT /api/auth/profile</code> with <code>{ college }</code>. The backend also accepts <code>name</code> and <code>studentId</code>, but the UI does not expose them.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q199. How is the role badge decided?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It reads <code>user.isAdmin</code> from the profile response: true shows "ADMINISTRATOR", false shows "STUDENT". This is display only; it does not grant any permissions.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q200. Why can the profile page still load for an admin?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Admins are stored in the same User collection and have a valid token, so the profile API returns their record. The page labels them as an administrator and shows their activity, which may be empty.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q201. How does the Review button in recent activity work?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Each activity stores a <code>resultId</code> when it comes from a mock-test submission. If present, the button navigates to <code>/test-review/:resultId</code>; otherwise the button is not rendered.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q202. Is the profile route protected?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;"><code>App.jsx</code> does not wrap <code>/profile</code> in <code>ProtectedRoute</code>. Instead, the page calls the protected profile API with whatever token exists. Without a valid token the API returns 401, and the page shows "Failed to load profile". Adding the route guard would give a cleaner redirect to login.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q203. What is missing from the profile page?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">There is no avatar upload, no password-change form, no email or phone editing, no account-deletion option and no direct edit of name/student ID. The "College saved" message is partly in Hinglish, which should be localised consistently.</p>
    
</div>

<!-- 3.20 CERTIFICATES -->
<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">Pages 3.20 - 3.21</div>
  <h2>My Certificates and Certificate View</h2>
  <div class="route" style="font-family:Consolas; font-size:9pt; color:#0f766e;">/certificates &nbsp; /certificate/:id &nbsp;|&nbsp; Protected &nbsp;|&nbsp; /api/certificate/*</div>

  <p>The certificate module lists a student's earned certificates and renders a printable certificate of course completion.</p>

  <h3>Sections on the page</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>My Certificates:</strong> heading, explanatory text, empty state, certificate cards with course, ID and issue date.</li>
    <li><strong>Certificate View:</strong> action bar, decorative certificate, brand, student details table, completion statement, certificate ID and date, signature line.</li>
    <li><strong>Print:</strong> a "Print / Save as PDF" button that calls <code>window.print()</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q204. How does a student earn a certificate?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The backend's <code>getCourseProgress</code> counts all notes for the course and all mock tests whose subject matches the course. The course is complete only when there is content and the student has read every note and attempted every test. <code>GET /api/certificate/progress/:course</code> checks this, creates the certificate if needed, and returns it.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q205. How are course names normalised?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;"><code>normalizeCourse</code> maps aliases to <code>BCA</code>, <code>BBA</code> or <code>BIOTECH</code>. For example, "BIOTECHNOLOGY" and "BIO" both become <code>BIOTECH</code>. Notes are matched by a case-insensitive regular expression, and tests are matched by subject patterns such as <code>/BIOTECH|BIOTECHNOLOGY|\bBIO\b/i</code>.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q206. How is the certificate ID generated?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It uses the format <code>ML-YYYYMMDD-XXXXXXXX</code>: the current date plus eight random hexadecimal characters from <code>crypto.randomBytes(4)</code>, converted to uppercase. The field is unique in the database.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q207. What data is copied onto the certificate?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Student name, college, phone, email, student ID, course, issue date and certificate ID. The page prefers values stored on the certificate and falls back to the current profile for any blank field, so updating a college later can still appear if the certificate did not store it.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q208. Can a student view another student's certificate?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">No. <code>GET /api/certificate/:id</code> looks up the certificate by ID and checks <code>userId === req.user._id</code>. A mismatch returns an error, and the page shows "Certificate not found".</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q209. How is the certificate downloaded as a PDF?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">There is no server-side PDF generator. The page is styled for print, and the button calls <code>window.print()</code>; the student then chooses "Save as PDF" in the browser's print dialog. This is simple and reliable, but the layout depends on the browser's print settings.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q210. Can duplicate certificates be created?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The progress endpoint checks for an existing certificate with the same <code>userId</code> and <code>course</code> before creating one. The unique <code>certificateId</code> also prevents duplicate IDs. However, there is no compound unique index on <code>userId + course</code>, so two simultaneous requests could theoretically race; a compound index would close that gap.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q211. What are the limitations of the certificate feature?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Completion is all-or-nothing: every note and every test in the course must be completed. There is no public certificate verification page, no QR authenticity check, no authorised signature image and no server-generated PDF. The "Back to Profile" link on the certificate page is labelled "My Certificates" but goes to <code>/profile</code>, which is a minor navigation inconsistency.</p>
    
</div>

<!-- 3.21 CHATBOT -->
<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">Page 3.22</div>
  <h2>AI Chatbot and Full-Page Assistant</h2>
  <div class="route" style="font-family:Consolas; font-size:9pt; color:#0f766e;">Floating widget on every page &nbsp;+&nbsp; /chatbot</div>

  <p>Mentor Lab includes a floating AI assistant and a full-screen version of the same chat. It supports English and Hindi, text-to-speech playback, a typing indicator and a shortcut to the full-page experience.</p>

  <h3>Sections on the page</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Floating launcher:</strong> bottom-corner button that opens a compact chat window.</li>
    <li><strong>Chat header:</strong> title "Mentor Lab Assistant", language selector, expand and close buttons.</li>
    <li><strong>Messages:</strong> user and bot bubbles, welcome message, typing indicator.</li>
    <li><strong>Composer:</strong> text input and send button.</li>
    <li><strong>Speak control:</strong> per-message playback using the browser voice or server TTS.</li>
    <li><strong>Full page:</strong> the same chat with a larger layout, timestamps and suggestion prompts.</li>
  </ul>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q212. Which AI service powers the chatbot?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The backend calls the Groq OpenAI-compatible chat-completions API at <code>https://api.groq.com/openai/v1/chat/completions</code> with the model <code>openai/gpt-oss-20b</code> and the server's <code>GROQ_API_KEY</code>. The frontend never receives the key.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q213. What is the chatbot's system prompt?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It positions the model as a friendly college guidance assistant for Mentor Lab, specialising in BCA, BBA and Biotech courses, exams and careers. It instructs short, direct, conversational answers of 2-4 sentences, no filler, and a strict language choice: English or Hindi in Devanagari only.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q214. How is the language selected?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The chat window has an English/Hindi selector. The value is sent as <code>language</code> in the request body. The backend adds a language instruction to both the system prompt and the user message, so a Hindi request is answered in Hindi.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q215. Does the chatbot remember previous messages?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">No. Each request sends only the current message and language. The server does not receive the conversation history, so follow-up questions that depend on context may be misunderstood. Adding a message array or a session ID would enable real multi-turn conversation.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q216. How does text-to-speech work?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">For English, the browser's <code>speechSynthesis</code> API reads the reply locally. For Hindi, the frontend splits the text into chunks of about 180 characters and sends each chunk to <code>POST /api/ai/tts</code>. The backend proxies Google Translate TTS, returns <code>audio/mpeg</code>, and the browser plays the chunks in order. It falls back to the browser's Hindi voice if needed.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q217. Why are the text chunks limited to 180 characters?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Google Translate's TTS endpoint has a practical request-length limit. Chunking at word boundaries avoids truncated audio and keeps each request small. The browser plays the chunks sequentially with <code>onended</code> callbacks.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q218. What is the /explain endpoint used for?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It is intended to explain a note or topic. It accepts a subject, course and language, tries to find a local PDF named after the subject, extracts up to 8,000 characters, and asks Groq for a simple explanation ending with key points. The frontend contains an <code>AIExplainModal</code> component for this, but it is not imported by any page, so the feature is currently backend-only.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q219. What is the biggest deployment bug in the chatbot?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Both <code>Chatbot.jsx</code> and <code>ChatbotPage.jsx</code> call <code>http://localhost:5000/api/ai/chat</code> and <code>http://localhost:5000/api/ai/tts</code> directly instead of using a relative <code>/api</code> path. This works only on the developer's machine; after deployment the browser will try to reach the visitor's own localhost. The URLs should be relative or read from an environment variable.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q220. Is the AI API authenticated or rate-limited?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">No. <code>/api/ai/chat</code>, <code>/explain</code> and <code>/tts</code> are public and have no per-user quota or rate limiter. This exposes the Groq key's quota to abuse. Authentication, request throttling and a maximum message length would be appropriate before public deployment.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q221. What are the security and privacy considerations of the AI feature?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Student messages are sent to a third-party AI provider, so the privacy policy should disclose this. The backend must never expose the Groq key. Prompt injection is possible because user text is passed directly, so the system prompt should be treated as guidance, not a security boundary. TTS also depends on an unofficial Google endpoint, which may change or block traffic.</p>
    
</div>

<!-- 3.22 CODE COMPILER -->
<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">Page 3.23</div>
  <h2>Online Code Compiler</h2>
  <div class="route" style="font-family:Consolas; font-size:9pt; color:#0f766e;">Embedded on /bca/programming-languages &nbsp;|&nbsp; Public</div>

  <p>The Code Compiler is a browser-based editor that lets a student write and run code without installing an IDE. It is embedded only in the BCA Programming Languages category.</p>

  <h3>Sections on the page</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Header:</strong> "Online Code Compiler" and a short description.</li>
    <li><strong>Language tabs:</strong> C, C++, Java, Python, JavaScript and HTML/CSS/JS.</li>
    <li><strong>Editor:</strong> a monospace textarea with a language-specific starter template.</li>
    <li><strong>Standard input:</strong> optional stdin box for programs that read input.</li>
    <li><strong>Run button:</strong> compiles and executes the code.</li>
    <li><strong>Output panel:</strong> stdout, compiler errors or runtime errors; HTML mode shows an iframe preview.</li>
  </ul>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q222. Which compiler service does the component use?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It sends a POST request to <code>https://wandbox.org/api/compile.json</code> with <code>{ code, compiler, stdin }</code>. Wandbox is a public online compilation service. The compiler IDs are <code>gcc-12.3.0-c</code> for C, <code>gcc-12.3.0</code> for C++, <code>openjdk-jdk-21+35</code> for Java, <code>cpython-3.11.10</code> for Python and <code>nodejs-20.17.0</code> for JavaScript.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q223. How does the component handle HTML/CSS/JS?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">HTML has no compiler ID. When Run is clicked, the code is placed directly into an <code>&lt;iframe sandbox="allow-scripts" srcDoc={code}&gt;</code>. The sandbox allows scripts but blocks same-origin access, which reduces the risk of the preview interfering with the parent page.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q224. How are output and errors displayed?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The component reads <code>program_output</code> for normal output. If <code>compiler_error</code> exists, it shows that; otherwise it checks <code>program_error</code>. If there is no output, it displays "(no output)". The output panel turns into an error style when an error is present.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q225. What happens when the student switches language?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;"><code>selectLanguage</code> updates the selected language, replaces the editor content with that language's starter template, and clears the output, error and HTML preview. This prevents Python code from being submitted as Java.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q226. Is the compiler integrated with the backend?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">No. The browser calls Wandbox directly. This keeps the backend simple, but it means the feature depends on a third-party service, is subject to CORS/network failures, and cannot be rate-limited or audited by the application server.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q227. Can a student save or submit code?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">No. Code lives only in React state. There is no save, no download, no submission history and no link to a mock-test question. Refreshing the page resets the editor to the default Python template.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q228. What are the limitations and risks of this compiler?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It requires an internet connection, depends on Wandbox availability and quotas, has no execution timeout shown to the user, and the HTML preview can run arbitrary JavaScript in a sandboxed iframe. For a real product, a self-hosted sandboxed runner (for example Judge0 or Piston) with CPU/memory/time limits would be safer and more controllable.</p>
    
</div>

<!-- 3.23 ADMIN DASHBOARD -->
<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">Page 3.24</div>
  <h2>Admin Dashboard</h2>
  <div class="route" style="font-family:Consolas; font-size:9pt; color:#0f766e;">/admin &nbsp;|&nbsp; AdminRoute + server checks &nbsp;|&nbsp; /api/admin, /api/notes, /api/pyq, /api/payment</div>

  <p>The dashboard is the control centre for content, students, tests and payments. It is a single-page layout with a fixed sidebar and seven sections.</p>

  <h3>Sections on the page</h3>
  <table border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="3" width="100%">
    <tbody><tr><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">Section</th><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">What the admin can do</th></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Overview</td><td valign="top">Stat cards and charts: platform overview, content distribution, registration trend, tests and questions by subject</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Mock Tests</td><td valign="top">Upload a PDF to auto-create a test, or add questions manually; set title, subject, topic, duration and price</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Students</td><td valign="top">View registered students with name, email, student ID and registration date</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Purchases</td><td valign="top">View all purchases, manually unlock a test for a student, confirm a pending payment</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Payment Settings</td><td valign="top">Set UPI ID, phone, payee name and upload a QR image; preview the current settings</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Upload Notes</td><td valign="top">Upload a PDF with title, description, course and subject; view and delete notes</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Upload PYQ</td><td valign="top">Upload a PYQ PDF with university, course and subject; view and delete papers</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q229. How is the admin dashboard protected?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The route is wrapped in <code>AdminRoute</code>, which requires a token and <code>isAdmin === "true"</code> in local storage. The sensitive write actions also send the token, and some server routes check <code>req.user.isAdmin</code>. However, several read-only admin endpoints such as <code>/api/admin/stats</code> and <code>/api/admin/students</code> currently have no <code>protect</code> or admin middleware, so this should be fixed.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q230. Which API provides the dashboard statistics?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;"><code>GET /api/admin/stats</code>. It returns counts of user accounts (including admins), courses, notes, lectures, tests and total questions, plus a subject breakdown and a six-month student-registration trend. The frontend renders these with Recharts pie/bar/line charts and stat cards.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q231. How does the admin add a mock test from a PDF?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The admin chooses "Upload PDF", fills in title, subject, topic, duration and price, and selects a PDF. The form posts to <code>POST /api/mocktest/upload-pdf</code>. The server stores the file, extracts text with pdf-parse, parses questions, options and answers, and creates a MockTest document. The PDF should follow a format such as "1. Question", "A) Option" and "Ans: A".</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q232. How does manual test creation work?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The admin chooses "Add Manually", enters the test metadata and one or more questions, each with four options and a correct answer. The form posts to <code>POST /api/mocktest/add</code>. The frontend validates that at least one question has text and all four options filled before submitting.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q233. How is a test unlocked manually?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">In the Purchases section, the admin selects a student and a test and submits <code>POST /api/admin/purchase/manual</code>. The route checks <code>req.user.isAdmin</code>, verifies that the test exists, and upserts a Purchase with status <code>manual</code>, amount equal to the test price and placeholder order/payment IDs.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q234. How does the admin confirm a pending payment?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The admin submits the purchase ID to <code>POST /api/admin/purchase/confirm</code>. The route verifies the admin role and updates the purchase status to <code>simulated</code>, which makes <code>hasAccess</code> return true. This is intended for manual UPI reconciliation.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q235. How are payment settings stored?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">They are stored in a single Setting document with key <code>payments</code>, containing UPI ID, phone, payee name and QR image path. The admin reads and writes through <code>GET/POST /api/payment/settings/admin</code>. The public <code>GET /api/payment/settings</code> is used by the student payment modal.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q236. How is the payment QR image handled?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The admin form uses Multer with disk storage and uploads the image to the backend <code>uploads</code> folder with a generated name such as <code>qr_*.png</code>. The old QR file is deleted when a new one is saved. The public settings response exposes a QR URL for the student modal.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q237. How does the admin upload study notes?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The dashboard form collects course, subject, title, description and a PDF. It posts multipart data to <code>POST /api/notes/upload</code>. The backend also supports plain web content, but the dashboard exposes only the PDF path. A PDF is stored in the <code>pdfs</code> GridFS bucket, its text is extracted with pdf-parse, and the note is saved with <code>fileId</code>, <code>content</code> and <code>hasContent</code>.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q238. How does the admin upload a PYQ?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The form collects university, course, subject and a PDF and posts to <code>POST /api/pyq/upload</code>. The file goes into the <code>pyqs</code> GridFS bucket, and a PYQ document stores the university ID/name, course, subject and file ID. The student portal then finds it by university ID and course/subject key.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q239. How does the admin delete notes or PYQs?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The dashboard calls <code>DELETE /api/notes/:id</code> or <code>DELETE /api/pyq/:id</code> with the token and refreshes the list. The routes require authentication but do not explicitly check <code>isAdmin</code>, so a logged-in student could call them directly. This should be hardened with an admin middleware.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q240. What are the main limitations of the admin dashboard?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Some read endpoints are public, role checks are inconsistent across modules, there is no pagination for large student/purchase lists, no search/filter on most tables, and no audit log. The UI is a large 1,000+ line component, so splitting each section into its own component would improve maintainability. Real Razorpay configuration and email templates are not managed from the dashboard.</p>
    
</div>

<!-- ============================ PART 4 ============================ -->

      <div class="page-break"></div>
      <h1 style="color:#0b1f3a;">Part 04 — Backend, API and Database Reference</h1>
      <p style="color:#40525e; font-size:10pt;">Technical reference for the viva: server startup, request flow, Mongoose models and the complete endpoint map.</p>
    

<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">4.1</div>
  <h2>Server Startup and Request Pipeline</h2>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q241. How does the backend start?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;"><code>backend/src/index.js</code> loads environment variables with <code>dotenv</code>, creates the Express app, enables <code>cors()</code> and <code>express.json()</code>, mounts the eight route modules under <code>/api</code>, serves the <code>uploads</code> folder, serves the built frontend from <code>frontend/dist</code>, calls <code>connectDB()</code>, and starts listening on <code>PORT</code> (default 5000). MongoDB connection is therefore initiated at startup, while the HTTP server can begin listening before the connection has fully completed.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q242. What is the full request lifecycle for an API call?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Browser → Vite dev proxy or production same-origin request → Express → CORS and JSON middleware → route module → optional <code>protect</code> / <code>isAdmin</code> middleware → controller → Mongoose model → MongoDB → JSON response. In development, Vite proxies <code>/api</code> and <code>/uploads</code> to <code>http://localhost:5000</code>, so the frontend can use relative URLs without CORS problems.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q243. How is the production frontend served?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">After the API routes, Express serves <code>frontend/dist</code> as static files. A catch-all route <code>app.get("/{*path}")</code> returns <code>index.html</code>, which allows React Router to handle deep links such as <code>/mock-test</code> or <code>/notes/123</code>. A build must exist in <code>frontend/dist</code>; otherwise the catch-all points to a missing file.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q244. What happens to an unknown /api URL?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Because the catch-all is registered after the API routers but matches every path, an unknown <code>/api/...</code> request can fall through to <code>index.html</code> instead of returning a JSON 404. This can confuse API clients. A dedicated <code>app.use("/api", ...)</code> 404 handler before the SPA fallback would make the behaviour correct.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q245. Is there a global error handler?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">No. Controllers use <code>try/catch</code> and return their own status codes and messages. Multer errors, malformed JSON, unexpected promise rejections and database connection failures are not normalised by a central middleware. A production app should add one error handler with consistent JSON shape and logging.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q246. How is CORS configured?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;"><code>app.use(cors())</code> allows every origin. This is convenient in development but too permissive for production, especially with token-based APIs. It should be restricted to the deployed frontend origin through an environment variable.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q247. How are files stored?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Two storage strategies are used. PDFs for notes and PYQs go into MongoDB GridFS buckets (<code>pdfs</code> and <code>pyqs</code>) using <code>multer.memoryStorage()</code>, so the file never needs a permanent disk path. Mock-test PDFs and the payment QR image use disk storage under <code>backend/uploads</code> with generated filenames. The uploads folder is served statically.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q248. Why use GridFS instead of saving PDFs on disk?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">GridFS keeps the file and its metadata in MongoDB, survives redeploys without a persistent disk, supports files larger than 16 MB by chunking, and lets the database record be deleted together with its file ID. The trade-off is extra database load and the need to clean up orphaned chunks manually.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q249. How are PDFs streamed to the browser?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The route opens a GridFS download stream by file ID, sets <code>Content-Type: application/pdf</code> and pipes the stream to the response. The static uploads middleware also sets <code>Content-Disposition: inline</code> for PDFs, so the browser previews rather than force-downloads them.</p>
    
</div>

<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">4.2</div>
  <h2>Database Models</h2>

  <table border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="3" width="100%">
    <tbody><tr><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">Model</th><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">Purpose and key fields</th></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">User</td><td valign="top">Student and admin accounts: firstName, middleName, lastName, full name, unique email, password hash, phone, student ID, college, isAdmin, reset fields, performanceStats, recentActivity, readNotes, certificate IDs.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Note</td><td valign="top">Study material: title, description, course, subject, type (pdf/web), extracted content, GridFS fileId, optional pdfUrl, hasContent flag.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">PYQ</td><td valign="top">Previous-year paper: universityId, universityName, course, subject, GridFS fileId, optional pdfUrl.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">MockTest</td><td valign="top">Test metadata and questions: title, subject, topic, duration, price, createdAt, array of question objects (question, four options, correctAnswer).</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Result</td><td valign="top">One submitted attempt: userId, testId, title, per-question answers, score, maxScore, attempted, correct and wrong counts, timestamps.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Purchase</td><td valign="top">Test entitlement: userId, testId, amount, status (pending/paid/simulated/manual), orderId, paymentId; unique compound index on userId + testId.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Certificate</td><td valign="top">Course completion record: userId, course, subject, unique certificateId, copied student details, score, issue date.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Setting</td><td valign="top">Key-value configuration; used for the public payment/UPI settings document.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Lecture</td><td valign="top">Legacy model for recorded lectures. The Recorded Lectures page does not use it; its content is static.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q250. Why is the password field marked select: false?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Mongoose excludes it from normal queries, so accidentally returning a user object does not leak the bcrypt hash. Login explicitly uses <code>.select("+password")</code> when it needs to compare the password.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q251. What is stored in performanceStats?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Tests taken, average score, rank and hours learnt. Tests taken is incremented by the add-activity endpoint; the other fields are present in the schema but are not automatically recomputed from results, which is why analytics calculates accuracy and trends from <code>recentActivity</code> instead.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q252. What is stored in readNotes?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">An array of Note ObjectIds. <code>POST /api/certificate/notes/:id/read</code> adds the note with <code>$addToSet</code>, which prevents duplicates. Certificate progress converts the array to a set, filters the course's notes against it, and compares the count with the course's total notes.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q253. How does the unique purchase index work?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">A compound unique index on <code>{ userId, testId }</code> guarantees that one student can own at most one purchase record per test. The order flow uses <code>findOneAndUpdate</code> with <code>upsert</code>, so retrying a payment updates the existing record instead of creating duplicates.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q254. Why does Result store the answers as an array instead of only a score?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The array preserves which option the student chose for each question, which is required by the Test Review page. It also makes the result auditable if a test's answer key is corrected later, although the current review page uses the stored <code>isCorrect</code> flags.</p>
    
</div>

<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">4.3</div>
  <h2>API Endpoint Map</h2>

  <table border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="3" width="100%">
    <tbody><tr><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">Method and path</th><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">Purpose</th><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">Access</th></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">POST <code>/api/auth/register</code></td><td valign="top">Create student account</td><td valign="top">Public</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">POST <code>/api/auth/login</code></td><td valign="top">Student or admin login</td><td valign="top">Public</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">POST <code>/api/auth/forgot-password</code></td><td valign="top">Request an OTP</td><td valign="top">Public</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">POST <code>/api/auth/verify-otp</code></td><td valign="top">Verify OTP and receive reset token</td><td valign="top">Public</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">POST <code>/api/auth/reset-password</code></td><td valign="top">Set a new password with reset token</td><td valign="top">Public</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">GET/PUT <code>/api/auth/profile</code></td><td valign="top">Read or update profile</td><td valign="top">Token</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">POST <code>/api/auth/add-activity</code></td><td valign="top">Record a test activity and increment tests taken</td><td valign="top">Token</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">GET <code>/api/notes</code>, <code>/api/notes/:id</code></td><td valign="top">List notes or read one note</td><td valign="top">Public</td></tr>
