    <tr><td valign="top">GET <code>/api/certificate/:id</code></td><td valign="top">View one owned certificate</td><td valign="top">Token + owner</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">POST <code>/api/ai/chat</code></td><td valign="top">Ask the AI assistant</td><td valign="top">Public</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">POST <code>/api/ai/explain</code></td><td valign="top">Explain a topic from notes</td><td valign="top">Public</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">POST <code>/api/ai/tts</code></td><td valign="top">Hindi text-to-speech proxy</td><td valign="top">Public</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">GET <code>/api/payment/settings</code></td><td valign="top">Public UPI settings</td><td valign="top">Public</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">GET/POST <code>/api/payment/settings/admin</code></td><td valign="top">Read/update payment settings</td><td valign="top">Token</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">GET <code>/api/admin/stats</code></td><td valign="top">Dashboard statistics</td><td valign="top">Currently public</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">GET/POST <code>/api/admin/courses</code></td><td valign="top">Course list / create course</td><td valign="top">Currently public</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">GET <code>/api/admin/students</code></td><td valign="top">Student list</td><td valign="top">Currently public</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">GET <code>/api/admin/purchases</code></td><td valign="top">Purchase list</td><td valign="top">Token + admin</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">POST <code>/api/admin/purchase/manual</code></td><td valign="top">Manually unlock a test</td><td valign="top">Token + admin</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">POST <code>/api/admin/purchase/confirm</code></td><td valign="top">Confirm a pending purchase</td><td valign="top">Token + admin</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q255. What is the standard response shape?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">There is no enforced envelope. Successful endpoints usually return the resource directly or an object such as <code>{ message, test }</code>; errors return <code>{ message }</code> with 400, 401, 403, 404 or 500. A consistent shape such as <code>{ success, data, error }</code> would make the frontend simpler.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q256. How does the frontend attach the token?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Each page reads <code>localStorage.getItem("token")</code> and sets an <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code> header with Axios or <code>fetch</code>. There is no global Axios interceptor, so every protected call must remember to add the header; an interceptor would remove this duplication.</p>
    
</div>

<!-- ============================ PART 5 ============================ -->

      <div class="page-break"></div>
      <h1 style="color:#0b1f3a;">Part 05 — Feature Deep Dives</h1>
      <p style="color:#40525e; font-size:10pt;">The flows an examiner is most likely to ask you to trace end-to-end, written as compact whiteboard sequences.</p>
    

<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">5.1</div>
  <h2>Student Registration and Login</h2>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q257. Trace registration from form to database.</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">1) The student submits name, email, phone, student ID, college and password. 2) The frontend validates required fields and local password rules. 3) <code>POST /api/auth/register</code> trims and validates the name, checks phone length for supported countries, checks the email domain against a free/fake-domain list, and rejects a duplicate email. 4) The password is hashed with bcrypt (10 rounds). 5) The user is created with <code>isAdmin: false</code>. 6) A JWT is signed for 7 days. 7) The response contains the user and token; the frontend stores both and logs the student in.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q258. Trace login.</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">1) <code>POST /api/auth/login</code> receives email and password. 2) The user is found by email with <code>.select("+password")</code>. 3) If no user or no password match, a generic "Invalid email or password" is returned. 4) A JWT is signed with <code>{ id: user._id }</code>. 5) The frontend stores the token and a sanitised user object. 6) <code>AdminLogin</code> performs the same call but rejects the response if <code>isAdmin</code> is not true.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q259. How does a protected request prove identity?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The <code>protect</code> middleware reads the Bearer token, verifies it with <code>JWT_SECRET</code>, loads the user by the decoded ID, attaches the user to <code>req.user</code> and calls <code>next()</code>. A missing or invalid token returns 401. Because the user is reloaded from MongoDB, deleting or demoting a user takes effect on the next request.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q260. How does password reset work?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">1) Forgot-password finds the user and enforces a 60-second resend cooldown. 2) A six-digit OTP is generated with <code>crypto.randomInt</code>, hashed with bcrypt, saved with a 10-minute expiry and an attempt counter, and emailed. 3) Verify-otp compares the hash, increments attempts on failure, and after success returns a short-lived reset JWT (10 minutes) whose ID is stored as a SHA-256 hash. 4) Reset-password verifies that JWT, checks the strong-password rules, hashes the new password and clears the reset fields.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q261. What are the main weaknesses in the auth flow?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Signup does not apply the same server-side strong-password check as reset; email verification is absent; there is no rate limiting on login or signup; tokens live in <code>localStorage</code>; and email casing is not normalised consistently, so <code>User@x.com</code> and <code>user@x.com</code> can be treated as different accounts depending on the path. These are concrete improvements to mention.</p>
    
</div>

<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">5.2</div>
  <h2>Mock Test: Selection to Score</h2>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q262. Trace a free test attempt.</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">1) <code>GET /api/mocktest</code> returns tests without answer keys. 2) The student clicks Start; <code>openTest</code> sees <code>price === 0</code> and loads the full test. 3) The exam screen initialises answers to -1 and starts the timer. 4) On submit, <code>POST /api/mocktest/:id/submit</code> receives an answers array. 5) The server recomputes correctness and score, creates a Result, and returns the result ID and summary. 6) The frontend records activity and shows the scorecard.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q263. Trace a paid test attempt.</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">1) The test card shows a price and a lock. 2) Start opens the payment modal. 3) If Razorpay is configured, the frontend requests an order and opens checkout; otherwise it shows the UPI QR and UTR field. 4) The backend verifies the Razorpay signature or accepts the simulated UTR and creates a Purchase. 5) <code>hasAccess</code> now returns true. 6) The student starts the test and the normal submit flow runs.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q264. Where exactly is the score calculated?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Inside the submit controller. It iterates over the test's questions, resolves the selected answer text with <code>q.options[answers[idx]]</code>, compares that text with <code>q.correctAnswer</code>, increments correct or wrong, and computes <code>score = correct * 4 - wrong</code>. It then stores the per-question result and the totals. The browser only displays what the server returns.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q265. Can a student call the submit endpoint twice?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Yes. There is no unique constraint on <code>user + test</code> in the Result model and no attempt limit, so each submit creates a new Result. This may be desirable for practice, but it means "best score" or "first attempt" logic must be defined if the product needs it.</p>
    
</div>

<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">5.3</div>
  <h2>Payment State Machine</h2>

  <table border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="3" width="100%">
    <tbody><tr><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">State</th><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">Meaning</th><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">Access?</th></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">No purchase</td><td valign="top">Student has not paid or unlocked the test</td><td valign="top">Only if price is zero</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top"><code>pending</code></td><td valign="top">Order created, payment not confirmed</td><td valign="top">No</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top"><code>paid</code></td><td valign="top">Razorpay signature verified</td><td valign="top">Yes</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top"><code>simulated</code></td><td valign="top">Manual UPI reference accepted or admin-confirmed</td><td valign="top">Yes</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top"><code>manual</code></td><td valign="top">Admin unlocked the test without payment</td><td valign="top">Yes</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q266. Why is the amount multiplied by 100?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Razorpay expects the smallest currency unit, paise for INR. A ₹49 test is sent as 4900. The stored Purchase amount remains the human-readable rupee value.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q267. What prevents a forged Razorpay success?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The server recomputes the HMAC-SHA256 signature using the secret key and the exact <code>orderId|paymentId</code> string. A forged browser response cannot produce the correct signature without the secret. The server also checks the order belongs to the expected test and user flow.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q268. What is the weak point of the simulated payment?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">A student can enter any non-empty UTR and receive access immediately. The server cannot validate the UPI transfer, so the flow relies on trust and later admin reconciliation. For a real business, a payment gateway webhook or manual approval before unlocking would be safer.</p>
    
</div>

<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">5.4</div>
  <h2>Certificate Eligibility</h2>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q269. What exactly counts as course completion?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">For the requested course: there must be at least one note and at least one test; every note must be in <code>readNotes</code>; and every matching test must have a Result for that user. If any condition fails, progress returns incomplete with the missing counts.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q270. How does the system decide which tests belong to a course?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">It uses a case-insensitive regular expression on the test's <code>subject</code>. BCA matches "BCA"; BBA matches "BBA"; Biotech matches "BIOTECH", "BIOTECHNOLOGY" or the standalone word "BIO". This is why subject naming discipline matters: a typo can exclude a test from the certificate requirement.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q271. When is the certificate actually created?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">On the progress request, not on a separate "issue" button. The first time a student completes the course and asks for progress, the backend creates the Certificate with its unique ID. Later requests return the existing record.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q272. Why is certificate creation not fully race-safe?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The check-then-create sequence is not atomic and there is no compound unique index on <code>userId + course</code>. Two simultaneous requests could both see "no certificate" and insert. The unique <code>certificateId</code> prevents identical IDs, not duplicate course certificates. A compound unique index or an upsert would fix it.</p>
    
</div>

<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">5.5</div>
  <h2>Note Ingestion Pipeline</h2>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q273. Trace a PDF upload to a readable note.</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">1) Admin selects a PDF and metadata. 2) Multer receives it in memory. 3) The backend opens an upload stream to the <code>pdfs</code> GridFS bucket and writes the buffer. 4) <code>pdf-parse</code> extracts text from the same buffer. 5) A Note document is created with the file ID, text, course, subject and <code>hasContent</code>. 6) The student list shows the note; the viewer renders the text and links the original PDF.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q274. What happens if text extraction fails?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The note can still be saved as a PDF-only record with empty content, so the viewer shows a message and offers the PDF download. A scanned PDF with no text layer will behave this way. OCR would be needed to make it searchable and readable in the HTML view.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q275. How is a note deleted?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The route deletes the GridFS file by <code>fileId</code> and then deletes the Note document. This keeps the bucket from accumulating orphaned files. If the document delete fails after the file delete, the record can point to a missing file, so a transaction or ordered cleanup with logging is preferable.</p>
    
</div>

<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">5.6</div>
  <h2>AI Request Flow</h2>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q276. Trace one chatbot message.</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">1) The student types a message and the component adds it to local state. 2) It POSTs <code>{ message, language }</code> to the AI chat endpoint. 3) The backend builds a system prompt plus a language instruction and calls Groq with the model and a temperature setting. 4) It extracts the assistant text and returns it. 5) The component adds the bot bubble and optionally speaks it.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q277. What is sent to Groq?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">A system message describing the assistant's role, the current user message and a language instruction. Conversation history is not sent, so the model has no memory of earlier turns. The API key is read from <code>GROQ_API_KEY</code> on the server and never exposed to the browser.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q278. How would you add multi-turn memory?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Keep a messages array in React, send the last N turns to the backend, and have the backend forward them as the OpenAI-style messages list. For persistence, store a conversation ID and messages in MongoDB, with a limit on history length and token usage.</p>
    
</div>

<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">5.7</div>
  <h2>Admin Content Workflow</h2>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q279. What is the normal content workflow?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Admin logs in → dashboard overview → upload notes or PYQs → create a mock test from a PDF or manually → confirm purchases if needed → students see the new content immediately because all lists are read live from the API. There is no draft/publish workflow or scheduled release.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q280. Why does the dashboard need a refresh after uploads?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Each section loads its own list when it mounts or when an action succeeds. There is no global cache or real-time subscription, so a newly uploaded item appears when the relevant section refetches. This is simple and predictable, but a shared data store would avoid duplicate loading logic.</p>
    
</div>

<!-- ============================ PART 6 ============================ -->

      <div class="page-break"></div>
      <h1 style="color:#0b1f3a;">Part 06 — Security, Limitations and Improvements</h1>
      <p style="color:#40525e; font-size:10pt;">What is done well, what should be fixed before launch, and a prioritised improvement roadmap.</p>
    

<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">6.1</div>
  <h2>Security Strengths</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Passwords are hashed with bcrypt and the hash is excluded from normal queries.</li>
    <li>JWTs are signed server-side and verified on every protected request.</li>
    <li>Mock-test answers are hidden from the list API and scoring is recomputed on the server.</li>
    <li>Result and certificate reads check ownership, not just authentication.</li>
    <li>OTP codes are hashed, expire in 10 minutes, have a resend cooldown and an attempt limit.</li>
    <li>Password-reset tokens are short-lived and tied to a stored token ID, making them one-time.</li>
    <li>Razorpay payments are verified with an HMAC signature.</li>
    <li>Note text is HTML-escaped before rendering.</li>
    <li>HTML code preview runs inside a sandboxed iframe.</li>
    <li>The Groq and payment secrets stay on the server.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">6.2</div>
  <h2>Security Gaps and Bugs</h2>

  <table border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="3" width="100%">
    <tbody><tr><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">Issue</th><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">Impact and fix</th></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Hard-coded admin credentials</td><td valign="top">The seed script creates a known admin account. Remove the literal credentials, require a strong password from an environment variable, and rotate the existing password. Never include the credential in documentation.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Inconsistent admin authorisation</td><td valign="top"><code>/api/admin/stats</code>, <code>/api/admin/students</code>, <code>/api/admin/courses</code> are public; notes/PYQ upload/delete and mock-test upload/add check only for a token. Add a reusable <code>isAdmin</code> middleware to every admin and content-write route.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Open CORS</td><td valign="top">Any origin can call the API. Restrict to the deployed frontend domain.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Token in localStorage</td><td valign="top">XSS can steal the token. Prefer an HTTP-only, Secure, SameSite cookie with CSRF protection, or at minimum a strict CSP and short-lived tokens.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">No rate limiting</td><td valign="top">Login, signup, OTP, AI and file uploads can be abused. Add per-IP and per-user limits, especially for AI and OTP.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Signup password policy</td><td valign="top">The strong-password function is enforced on reset but not on registration. Apply it in both places.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">No email verification</td><td valign="top">Anyone can register with an address they do not own. Add a verification link or OTP before full access.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Upload validation is minimal</td><td valign="top">Multer has no MIME-type filter or file-size limit. A user can upload large or unexpected files. Add a PDF-only filter, a size cap and a safe filename.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Memory uploads</td><td valign="top">A very large PDF is held in RAM before GridFS. Set a limit to avoid memory exhaustion.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">No global error handler</td><td valign="top">Unexpected errors can leak stack traces or return HTML. Add central JSON error handling and avoid exposing internals.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Public AI endpoints</td><td valign="top">The Groq quota can be consumed by anyone. Require a token, limit message length and add rate limiting.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Prompt injection</td><td valign="top">User text can try to override the system prompt. Treat the system prompt as behaviour guidance, not a security boundary, and filter sensitive requests.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Hard-coded localhost URLs</td><td valign="top">The chatbot breaks after deployment. Use relative <code>/api</code> paths or an environment variable.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Certificate race</td><td valign="top">Two simultaneous progress calls can create duplicates. Add a compound unique index on user + course and use an upsert.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Manual UTR trust</td><td valign="top">A fake reference can unlock a test. Require admin approval or a gateway webhook.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">No audit log</td><td valign="top">Admin unlocks and content deletions are not attributable. Record actor, action, target and timestamp.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">No pagination</td><td valign="top">Student, purchase and content lists grow without bound. Add page/limit and indexes.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">No attempt limit / autosave</td><td valign="top">Students can submit repeatedly and lose answers on refresh. Define attempt rules and persist progress.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q281. If you had one day before launch, what would you fix first?</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Remove the hard-coded admin credential, add <code>isAdmin</code> to every admin/content-write route, restrict CORS, add upload limits and rate limiting, fix the chatbot's localhost URLs, and apply the strong-password check to signup. These are high-impact, low-effort fixes.</p>
    
</div>

<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">6.3</div>
  <h2>Code Quality and Maintainability</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>AdminDashboard.jsx</code> is very large; split it into one component per section.</li>
    <li><code>components/Sidebar.jsx</code> and <code>AdminCard.jsx</code> are empty files; remove them or implement them.</li>
    <li><code>AIExplainModal.jsx</code> is unused, and <code>BCARoadmap.jsx</code> duplicates <code>roadmapbca.jsx</code>; delete dead code.</li>
    <li>Repeated Axios/fetch logic and token handling should move into a shared API client with interceptors.</li>
    <li>Branding is inconsistent: "Mentor Lab" in the UI, "Drona Gyan" in the footer and <code>drona-gyan</code> in <code>package.json</code>.</li>
    <li>Some UI copy mixes English and Hinglish; choose one tone or localise properly.</li>
    <li>Date/number formatting and score parsing are duplicated across analytics, profile and certificates; extract helpers.</li>
    <li>Add unit tests for scoring, certificate eligibility, password reset and payment verification; add integration tests for the main API flows.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">6.4</div>
  <h2>Prioritised Roadmap</h2>

  <table border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="3" width="100%">
    <tbody><tr><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">Priority</th><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">Work</th></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">P0</td><td valign="top">Remove seeded credentials; add admin middleware everywhere; fix localhost AI URLs; add upload limits and rate limits; restrict CORS.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">P1</td><td valign="top">HTTP-only cookies, email verification, signup password policy, pagination, API error envelope, compound certificate index, server-side exam autosave.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">P2</td><td valign="top">Dedicated analytics endpoint, lecture CMS, backend code runner, certificate verification page/QR, admin audit log, real-time dashboard.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">P3</td><td valign="top">Search and filters, accessibility audit, automated tests, CI/CD, observability, backup/restore plan.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
</div>

<!-- ============================ PART 7 ============================ -->

      <div class="page-break"></div>
      <h1 style="color:#0b1f3a;">Part 07 — Rapid-Fire Answers and Demo Script</h1>
      <p style="color:#40525e; font-size:10pt;">Last-minute revision, thirty one-line answers, likely viva traps and a ten-minute live demonstration plan.</p>
    

<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">7.1</div>
  <h2>Thirty Rapid-Fire Questions</h2>

  <table border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="3" width="100%">
    <tbody><tr><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">Question</th><th valign="top" bgcolor="#e8f1ef">Answer</th></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">What is the project?</td><td valign="top">A MERN education platform for BCA, BBA and Biotech students with notes, PYQs, mock tests, certificates, analytics and AI help.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">What does MERN stand for?</td><td valign="top">MongoDB, Express, React, Node.js.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Which database is used?</td><td valign="top">MongoDB through Mongoose.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Why MongoDB?</td><td valign="top">Flexible documents suit nested test questions, activity arrays and varied content metadata.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">How is the frontend built?</td><td valign="top">React 19 with Vite, React Router and plain/CSS modules plus some component-level styles.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">How does the frontend talk to the backend?</td><td valign="top">Axios/fetch to <code>/api</code>, proxied by Vite in development.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">How are passwords stored?</td><td valign="top">bcrypt hashes with 10 salt rounds; never plain text.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">What is JWT used for?</td><td valign="top">Stateless authentication; the token carries the user ID and expires in 7 days.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Where is the token stored?</td><td valign="top"><code>localStorage</code>, sent as a Bearer header.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">How is admin access checked?</td><td valign="top">Client-side <code>AdminRoute</code> plus <code>req.user.isAdmin</code> on some server routes; coverage is inconsistent and must be fixed.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">How does signup work?</td><td valign="top">Validate, check duplicate email, hash password, create user, return JWT.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">How does password reset work?</td><td valign="top">Email OTP → verify → short-lived reset JWT → set new password.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">How long is the OTP valid?</td><td valign="top">10 minutes, with a 60-second resend cooldown and limited attempts.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">How are notes stored?</td><td valign="top">PDF in GridFS plus extracted text in the Note document.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">How is note text rendered?</td><td valign="top"><code>renderPlainText</code> escapes HTML and converts plain text to paragraphs/headings.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">How are PYQs organised?</td><td valign="top">By university, then course and subject; PDFs come from the <code>pyqs</code> GridFS bucket.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">How is mock-test access controlled?</td><td valign="top">Free or purchased; <code>hasAccess</code> checks price and the Purchase collection.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">How is a paid test unlocked?</td><td valign="top">Razorpay signature verification or manual UPI UTR/admin unlock.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">How is the mock test scored?</td><td valign="top">+4 correct, -1 wrong, 0 unattempted, calculated on the server.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Why server-side scoring?</td><td valign="top">Prevents answer-key leakage and client-side score tampering.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">How is the exam timed?</td><td valign="top">A one-second interval decrements the remaining seconds and auto-submits at zero.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">What is the question palette?</td><td valign="top">Green answered, red unanswered, yellow marked for review.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">How is a certificate earned?</td><td valign="top">Read every note and attempt every test in the course.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">What is the certificate ID format?</td><td valign="top"><code>ML-YYYYMMDD-8 hex characters</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">How is the certificate downloaded?</td><td valign="top">Browser print dialog → Save as PDF.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Which AI provider is used?</td><td valign="top">Groq's OpenAI-compatible API with <code>openai/gpt-oss-20b</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">How does Hindi TTS work?</td><td valign="top">Text is chunked and proxied through Google Translate TTS; English uses browser speech synthesis.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">Which compiler service is used?</td><td valign="top">Wandbox, called directly from the browser.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">What is GridFS?</td><td valign="top">MongoDB's specification for storing large files as chunks plus metadata.</td></tr>
    <tr><td valign="top">What would you improve first?</td><td valign="top">Admin authorisation, seeded credentials, rate limiting, upload validation and deployment URLs.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
</div>

<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">7.2</div>
  <h2>Ten-Minute Demonstration Script</h2>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>Pitch (30s):</strong> "Mentor Lab is a MERN learning platform for BCA, BBA and Biotech students. It combines structured notes, previous-year papers, paid and free mock tests, progress analytics, certificates and an AI study assistant."</li>
    <li><strong>Home and navigation (45s):</strong> Show the hero, courses, QR signup, navbar and the three course areas. Mention that public pages need no login.</li>
    <li><strong>Student signup/login (45s):</strong> Register or log in, show the token/user in local storage, and explain JWT and bcrypt in one sentence each.</li>
    <li><strong>Notes (60s):</strong> Open Study Notes → BCA → a category → a note. Show the TOC, reading progress and original PDF link. Explain GridFS and text extraction.</li>
    <li><strong>PYQ (45s):</strong> Choose a university, show the course columns, and open a paper. Explain the GridFS <code>pyqs</code> bucket.</li>
    <li><strong>Mock test (2 min):</strong> Start a free test, answer a few questions, mark one for review, show the palette and timer, submit, and show the scorecard. Explain +4/-1 and server-side scoring.</li>
    <li><strong>Paid test (45s):</strong> Open a paid test, show the payment modal, explain Razorpay HMAC verification and the UPI fallback. Do not perform a real payment.</li>
    <li><strong>Review and analytics (45s):</strong> Open Test Review, then Analytics. Explain accuracy, trend and subject grouping from activity data.</li>
    <li><strong>Certificate (45s):</strong> Show the eligibility rule and a certificate view; use Print → Save as PDF.</li>
    <li><strong>AI and compiler (45s):</strong> Ask the chatbot a course question, switch to Hindi, and run a small Python/C program in the compiler.</li>
    <li><strong>Admin (60s):</strong> Log in as admin, show stats, upload a note or test, and show the student/purchase sections. Mention that some admin endpoints still need hardening.</li>
    <li><strong>Close (30s):</strong> Summarise the architecture, the strongest features and the P0 improvements. End with one measurable future goal.</li>
  </ol>
</div>

<div class="page-break"></div><div class="section">
  <div class="kicker">7.3</div>
  <h2>Likely Viva Traps</h2>

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q282. "If the score is calculated in React, can I change it?"</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">No. The result shown is what the server returned. The submit endpoint recomputes from the database test, and the list endpoint omits <code>correctAnswer</code>. The frontend only renders the response.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q283. "Why not store the JWT in a cookie?"</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">A cookie can be HTTP-only, so JavaScript cannot read it, which reduces XSS token theft. It needs CSRF protection and correct SameSite/Secure settings. The current localStorage approach is simpler but weaker.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q284. "Is the AI generating the quiz questions?"</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">No. Tests are created by the admin manually or by parsing an uploaded PDF. The AI is only used for chat, explanations and text-to-speech.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q285. "How do you know a student completed the course?"</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The backend compares the course's note count with the student's <code>readNotes</code>, and the course's test count with the student's Result records. Both must match and be non-zero.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q286. "What happens if two students have the same name?"</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Email is the unique login identifier. Names are not unique; student ID is stored for display but is not used as the authentication key.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q287. "Can the app run without internet?"</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">The main MERN app can run locally, but the AI chatbot, Hindi TTS and code compiler need internet because they call Groq, Google and Wandbox.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q288. "What is the biggest limitation of the project?"</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">Authorisation consistency and production hardening. The feature set is broad, but admin APIs, uploads, rate limits and deployment URLs need tightening before a public launch.</p>
    

  
      <p style="margin-top:8pt; margin-bottom:2pt;"><strong>Q289. "What did you learn?"</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:0; margin-bottom:8pt;">How to connect a React SPA to an Express/MongoDB API, model paid access and scoring securely, handle file storage with GridFS, and reason about authentication, ownership and third-party integrations.</p>
    
</div>

<p style="margin-top:14pt;"><strong>
  <strong>Final one-line pitch:</strong> Mentor Lab is a full-stack learning platform that brings curriculum notes, previous-year papers, practice tests, payments, certificates, analytics and AI assistance into one student workspace.
</strong></p>



</div></body></html>